== Ad Matutinum (Tenebræ) | Matins (Tenebrae)
H: FERIA QUINTA IN CENA DOMINI
 = MAUNDY THURSDAY
H: AD MATUTINUM
 = MATINS
S: Ecclesiasticum Officium O.P. Triduo ante Pascha (1927), pp. 1-31
R: Officium celebratur ad modum Festi Duplicis. Feria V in Cena Domini et duabus sequentibus, Prior (ex choro dextro) faciat Officium ad Horas in quibus in festis solemnibus est notatum. In Matutinis quindecim candelæ coram altari præparentur et ante Matutinum accendantur; quarum singulæ ad inceptionem cujuslibet Psalmi, una hinc et alia inde, a Sacrista exstinguantur. Similiter fiat in Feria sexta et Sabbato.
 = The Office is celebrated in the manner of a Double feast. On Maundy Thursday and the two following days the Prior (from the right-hand choir) does the Office at the Hours at which he does it on solemn feasts. At Matins fifteen candles are set before the altar and lit before Matins; one of them is put out by the Sacristan at the beginning of each Psalm, one on this side and one on the other. The same is done on Good Friday and Holy Saturday.
R: Hoc triduo ante Matutinum non dicatur *Dómine, lábia mea apéries,* nec *Deus, in adjutórium,* neque Invitatórium, neque Hymnus; sed dicto *Pater noster,* et *Credo in Deum,* postquam versis vultibus ad altare signaverint se Fratres, statim ex choro dextro incipiatur:
 = On these three days *Dómine, lábia mea apéries,* *Deus, in adjutórium,* the Invitatory and the Hymn are not said before Matins; but when the *Pater noster* and the *Credo* have been said, and the brethren, turned towards the altar, have signed themselves, the right-hand choir at once begins:
R: Non dicatur *Glória Patri* in fine Psalmorum et Canticorum. Ultimi Versus Psalmorum et Canticorum secundum tonorum differentias terminentur sicut et alii.
 = The *Glória Patri* is not said at the end of the Psalms and Canticles. The last verses of the Psalms and Canticles end according to the endings of the tones, like the others.
H: IN I NOCTURNO
 = FIRST NOCTURN
T: {Aña} Zelus {Ps. 68} Salvum me fac, Deus.
 = {Ant.} The zeal… {Ps. 68}
F: Psalmus 68 Salvum me fac, Deus | Psalm 68
G: op-ten-h-ps-68 | Tonus VIII c
F: Textus et versio | Text and translation
T: Salvum me fac, Deus: * quóniam intravérunt aquæ usque ad ánimam meam.
 = Save me, O God: * for the waters are come in even unto my soul.
T: Infíxus sum in limo profúndi: * et non est substántia.
 = I stick fast in the mire of the deep: * and there is no sure standing.
T: Veni in altitúdinem maris: * et tempéstas demérsit me.
 = I am come into the depth of the sea: * and a tempest hath overwhelmed me.
T: Laborávi clamans, raucæ factæ sunt fauces meæ: * defecérunt óculi mei, dum spero in Deum meum.
 = I have laboured with crying; my jaws are become hoarse: * my eyes have failed, whilst I hope in my God.
T: Multiplicáti sunt super capíllos cápitis mei, * qui odérunt me gratis.
 = They are multiplied above the hairs of my head, * who hate me without cause.
T: Confortáti sunt qui persecúti sunt me inimíci mei iniúste: * quæ non rápui, tunc exsolvébam.
 = My enemies are grown strong who have wrongfully persecuted me: * then did I pay that which I took not away.
T: Deus, tu scis insipiéntiam meam: * et delícta mea a te non sunt abscóndita.
 = O God, thou knowest my foolishness; * and my offences are not hidden from thee.
T: Non erubéscant in me qui exspéctant te, Dómine, * Dómine virtútum.
 = Let not them be ashamed for me, who look for thee, O Lord, * the Lord of hosts.
T: Non confundántur super me * qui quærunt te, Deus Israël.
 = Let them not be confounded on my account, * who seek thee, O God of Israel.
T: Quóniam propter te sustínui oppróbrium: * opéruit confúsio fáciem meam.
 = Because for thy sake I have borne reproach; * shame hath covered my face.
T: Extráneus factus sum frátribus meis, * et peregrínus fíliis matris meæ.
 = I am become a stranger to my brethren, * and an alien to the sons of my mother.
T: Quóniam zelus domus tuæ comédit me: * et oppróbria exprobrántium tibi cecidérunt super me.
 = For the zeal of thy house hath eaten me up: * and the reproaches of them that reproached thee are fallen upon me.
T: Et opérui in ieiúnio ánimam meam: * et factum est in oppróbrium mihi.
 = And I covered my soul in fasting: * and it was made a reproach to me.
T: Et pósui vestiméntum meum cilícium: * et factus sum illis in parábolam.
 = And I made haircloth my garment: * and I became a byword to them.
T: Advérsum me loquebántur, qui sedébant in porta: * et in me psallébant qui bibébant vinum.
 = They that sat in the gate spoke against me: * and they that drank wine made me their song.
T: Ego vero oratiónem meam ad te, Dómine: * tempus benepláciti, Deus.
 = But as for me, my prayer is to thee, O Lord; * for the time of thy good pleasure, O God.
T: In multitúdine misericórdiæ tuæ exáudi me, * in veritáte salútis tuæ:
 = In the multitude of thy mercy hear me, * in the truth of thy salvation.
T: Éripe me de luto, ut non infígar: * líbera me ab iis, qui odérunt me, et de profúndis aquárum.
 = Draw me out of the mire, that I may not stick fast: * deliver me from them that hate me, and out of the deep waters.
T: Non me demérgat tempéstas aquæ, neque absórbeat me profúndum: * neque úrgeat super me púteus os suum.
 = Let not the tempest of water drown me, nor the deep swallow me up: * and let not the pit shut her mouth upon me.
T: Exáudi me, Dómine, quóniam benígna est misericórdia tua: * secúndum multitúdinem miseratiónum tuárum réspice in me.
 = Hear me, O Lord, for thy mercy is kind; * look upon me according to the multitude of thy tender mercies.
T: Et ne avértas fáciem tuam a púero tuo: * quóniam tríbulor, velóciter exáudi me.
 = And turn not away thy face from thy servant: * for I am in trouble, hear me speedily.
T: Inténde ánimæ meæ, et líbera eam: * propter inimícos meos éripe me.
 = Attend to my soul, and deliver it: * save me because of my enemies.
T: Tu scis impropérium meum, et confusiónem meam, * et reveréntiam meam.
 = Thou knowest my reproach, and my confusion, * and my shame.
T: In conspéctu tuo sunt omnes qui tríbulant me: * impropérium exspectávit cor meum, et misériam.
 = In thy sight are all they that afflict me; * my heart hath expected reproach and misery.
T: Et sustínui qui simul contristarétur, et non fuit: * et qui consolarétur, et non invéni.
 = And I looked for one that would grieve together with me, * but there was none: and for one that would comfort me, and I found none.
T: Et dedérunt in escam meam fel: * et in siti mea potavérunt me acéto.
 = And they gave me gall for my food, * and in my thirst they gave me vinegar to drink.
T: Fiat mensa eórum coram ipsis in láqueum, * et in retributiónes, et in scándalum.
 = Let their table become as a snare before them, * and a recompense, and a stumblingblock.
T: Obscuréntur óculi eórum ne vídeant: * et dorsum eórum semper incúrva.
 = Let their eyes be darkened that they see not; * and their back bend thou down always.
T: Effúnde super eos iram tuam: * et furor iræ tuæ comprehéndat eos.
 = Pour out thy indignation upon them: * and let thy wrathful anger take hold of them.
T: Fiat habitátio eórum desérta: * et in tabernáculis eórum non sit qui inhábitet.
 = Let their habitation be made desolate: * and let there be none to dwell in their tabernacles.
T: Quóniam quem tu percussísti, persecúti sunt: * et super dolórem vúlnerum meórum addidérunt.
 = Because they have persecuted him whom thou hast smitten; * and they have added to the grief of my wounds.
T: Appóne iniquitátem super iniquitátem eórum: * et non intrent in iustítiam tuam.
 = Add thou iniquity upon their iniquity: * and let them not come into thy justice.
T: Deleántur de libro vivéntium: * et cum iustis non scribántur.
 = Let them be blotted out of the book of the living; * and with the just let them not be written.
T: Ego sum pauper et dolens: * salus tua, Deus, suscépit me.
 = I am poor and sorrowful: * thy salvation, O God, hath set me up.
T: Laudábo nomen Dei cum cántico: * et magnificábo eum in laude:
 = I will praise the name of God with a canticle: * and I will magnify him with praise.
T: Et placébit Deo super vítulum novéllum: * córnua producéntem et úngulas.
 = And it shall please God better than a young calf, * that bringeth forth horns and hoofs.
T: Vídeant páuperes et læténtur: * quǽrite Deum, et vivet ánima vestra.
 = Let the poor see and rejoice: * seek ye God, and your soul shall live.
T: Quóniam exaudívit páuperes Dóminus: * et vinctos suos non despéxit.
 = For the Lord hath heard the poor: * and hath not despised his prisoners.
T: Laudent illum cæli et terra, * mare et ómnia reptília in eis.
 = Let the heavens and the earth praise him; * the sea, and every thing that creepeth therein.
T: Quóniam Deus salvam fáciet Sion: * et ædificabúntur civitátes Iuda.
 = For God will save Sion, * and the cities of Juda shall be built up.
T: Et inhabitábunt ibi, * et hereditáte acquírent eam.
 = And they shall dwell there, * and acquire it by inheritance.
T: Et semen servórum eius possidébit eam: * et qui díligunt nomen eius, habitábunt in ea.
 = And the seed of his servants shall possess it; * and they that love his name shall dwell therein.
F/
F/
T: {Aña} Zelus domus tuæ comédit me, et oppróbria exprobrántium tibi cecidérunt super me.
 = {Ant.} The zeal of thy house hath eaten me up, and the reproaches of them that reproached thee are fallen upon me.
G: op-ten-h-t01 | Antiphona 1 · VIII
T: {Aña} Avertántur {Ps. 69} Deus, in adjutórium.
 = {Ant.} Let them… {Ps. 69}
F: Psalmus 69 Deus, in adjutórium | Psalm 69
G: op-ten-h-ps-69 | Tonus VIII c
F: Textus et versio | Text and translation
T: Deus, in adiutórium meum inténde: * Dómine, ad adiuvándum me festína.
 = O God, come to my assistance; * O Lord, make haste to help me.
T: Confundántur et revereántur, * qui quærunt ánimam meam.
 = Let them be confounded and ashamed * that seek my soul:
T: Avertántur retrórsum, et erubéscant, * qui volunt mihi mala.
 = Let them be turned backward, and blush for shame * that desire evils to me:
T: Avertántur statim erubescéntes, * qui dicunt mihi: Euge, euge.
 = Let them be presently turned away blushing for shame * that say to me: ’T is well, ’t is well.
T: Exsúltent et læténtur in te omnes qui quærunt te, * et dicant semper: Magnificétur Dóminus: qui díligunt salutáre tuum.
 = Let all that seek thee rejoice and be glad in thee; * and let such as love thy salvation say always: The Lord be magnified.
T: Ego vero egénus, et pauper sum: * Deus, ádiuva me.
 = But I am needy and poor; * O God, help me.
T: Adiútor meus, et liberátor meus es tu: * Dómine, ne moréris.
 = Thou art my helper and my deliverer: * O Lord, make no delay.
F/
F/
T: {Aña} Avertántur retrórsum, et erubéscant, qui cógitant mihi mala.
 = {Ant.} Let them be turned backward and blush for shame that desire evils to me.
G: op-ten-h-t02 | Antiphona 2 · VIII
T: {Aña} Deus {Ps. 70} In te, Dómine, sperávi.
 = {Ant.} Deliver me,… {Ps. 70}
F: Psalmus 70 In te, Dómine, sperávi | Psalm 70
G: op-ten-h-ps-70 | Tonus VIII c
F: Textus et versio | Text and translation
T: In te, Dómine, sperávi, non confúndar in ætérnum: * in iustítia tua líbera me, et éripe me.
 = In thee, O Lord, I have hoped, let me never be put to confusion: * deliver me in thy justice, and rescue me.
T: Inclína ad me aurem tuam, * et salva me.
 = Incline thy ear unto me, * and save me.
T: Esto mihi in Deum protectórem, et in locum munítum: * ut salvum me fácias,
 = Be thou unto me a God, a protector, and a place of strength: * that thou mayst make me safe.
T: Quóniam firmaméntum meum, * et refúgium meum es tu.
 = For thou art my firmament * and my refuge.
T: Deus meus, éripe me de manu peccatóris, * et de manu contra legem agéntis et iníqui:
 = Deliver me, O my God, out of the hand of the sinner, * and out of the hand of the transgressor of the law and of the unjust.
T: Quóniam tu es patiéntia mea, Dómine: * Dómine, spes mea a iuventúte mea.
 = For thou art my patience, O Lord: * my hope, O Lord, from my youth.
T: In te confirmátus sum ex útero: * de ventre matris meæ tu es protéctor meus.
 = By thee have I been confirmed from the womb: * from my mother’s womb thou art my protector.
T: In te cantátio mea semper: * tamquam prodígium factus sum multis: et tu adiútor fortis.
 = Of thee shall I continually sing: * I am become unto many as a wonder, but thou art a strong helper.
T: Repleátur os meum laude, ut cantem glóriam tuam: * tota die magnitúdinem tuam.
 = Let my mouth be filled with praise, that I may sing thy glory; * thy greatness all the day long.
T: Ne proícias me in témpore senectútis: * cum defécerit virtus mea, ne derelínquas me.
 = Cast me not off in the time of old age: * when my strength shall fail, do not thou forsake me.
T: Quia dixérunt inimíci mei mihi: * et qui custodiébant ánimam meam, consílium fecérunt in unum.
 = For my enemies have spoken against me; * and they that watched my soul have consulted together,
T: Dicéntes: Deus derelíquit eum, persequímini, et comprehéndite eum: * quia non est qui erípiat.
 = Saying: God hath forsaken him: pursue and take him, * for there is none to deliver him.
T: Deus, ne elongéris a me: * Deus meus, in auxílium meum réspice.
 = O God, be not thou far from me: * O my God, make haste to my help.
T: Confundántur, et defíciant detrahéntes ánimæ meæ: * operiántur confusióne, et pudóre qui quærunt mala mihi.
 = Let them be confounded and come to nothing that detract my soul; * let them be covered with confusion and shame that seek my hurt.
T: Ego autem semper sperábo: * et adíciam super omnem laudem tuam.
 = But I will always hope; * and will add to all thy praise.
T: Os meum annuntiábit iustítiam tuam: * tota die salutáre tuum.
 = My mouth shall shew forth thy justice; * thy salvation all the day long.
T: Quóniam non cognóvi litteratúram, introíbo in poténtias Dómini: * Dómine, memorábor iustítiæ tuæ solíus.
 = Because I have not known learning, I will enter into the powers of the Lord: * O Lord, I will be mindful of thy justice alone.
T: Deus, docuísti me a iuventúte mea: * et usque nunc pronuntiábo mirabília tua.
 = Thou hast taught me, O God, from my youth: * and till now I will declare thy wonderful works.
T: Et usque in senéctam et sénium: * Deus, ne derelínquas me,
 = And unto old age and grey hairs: * O God, forsake me not,
T: Donec annúntiem brácchium tuum * generatióni omni, quæ ventúra est:
 = Until I shew forth thy arm * to all the generation that is to come:
T: Poténtiam tuam, et iustítiam tuam, Deus, usque in altíssima, quæ fecísti magnália: * Deus, quis símilis tibi?
 = Thy power, and thy justice, O God, even to the highest great things thou hast done: * O God, who is like to thee?
T: Quantas ostendísti mihi tribulatiónes multas et malas: et convérsus vivificásti me: * et de abýssis terræ íterum reduxísti me:
 = How great troubles hast thou shewn me, many and grievous: and turning thou hast brought me to life, * and hast brought me back again from the depths of the earth:
T: Multiplicásti magnificéntiam tuam: * et convérsus consolátus es me.
 = Thou hast multiplied thy magnificence; * and turning to me thou hast comforted me.
T: Nam et ego confitébor tibi in vasis psalmi veritátem tuam: * Deus, psallam tibi in cíthara, Sanctus Israël.
 = For I will also confess to thee thy truth with the instruments of psaltery: * O God, I will sing to thee with the harp, thou holy one of Israel.
T: Exsultábunt lábia mea cum cantávero tibi: * et ánima mea, quam redemísti.
 = My lips shall greatly rejoice, when I shall sing to thee; * and my soul which thou hast redeemed.
T: Sed et lingua mea tota die meditábitur iustítiam tuam: * cum confúsi et revériti fúerint, qui quærunt mala mihi.
 = Yea and my tongue shall meditate on thy justice all the day; * when they shall be confounded and put to shame that seek evils to me.
F/
F/
T: {Aña} Deus meus, éripe me de manu peccatóris.
 = {Ant.} Deliver me, O my God, out of the hand of the sinner.
G: op-ten-h-t03 | Antiphona 3 · VIII
G: op-ten-h-v01 | Versus
T: {℣.} Exsúrge, Dómine. {℟.} Et júdica causam meam.
 = {℣.} Arise, O Lord. {℟.} And judge my cause.
R: Dicto *Pater noster* secreto, non pronuntietur *Et ne nos,* sed facto signo a Prælato, Lector incipiat legere; et hoc etiam fiat ante quartam et septimam Lectionem. *Jube, domne,* et *Tu autem,* non dicantur, sed finita Lectione sive Lamentatione, immediate inchoëtur Responsorium.
 = The *Pater noster* is said silently, and *Et ne nos* is not said aloud; but at a sign from the Prelate the Reader begins to read; and the same is done before the fourth and seventh Lessons. *Jube, domne,* and *Tu autem* are not said; when the Lesson or Lamentation is finished the Responsory is begun at once.
L: Lectio I
 = Lesson 1
I: De Lamentatione Jeremíæ Prophétæ (Lam. 1, 1-3)
 = Lamentations 1:1-3
G: op-ten-h-l01 | Lamentatio
F: Textus et versio | Text and translation
T: ^Incipit Lamentátio Jeremíæ Prophétæ. Aleph. Quómodo sedet sola cívitas plena pópulo: facta est quasi vídua dómina géntium: princeps provinciárum facta est sub tribúto. Beth. Plorans plorávit in nocte, et lácrimæ ejus in maxíllis ejus: non est qui consolétur eam ex ómnibus caris ejus: omnes amíci ejus sprevérunt eam, et facti sunt ei inimíci. Ghimel. Migrávit Judas propter afflictiónem, et multitúdinem servitútis: habitávit inter gentes, nec invénit réquiem: omnes persecutóres ejus apprehendérunt eam inter angústias. Jerúsalem, Jerúsalem, convértere ad Dóminum Deum tuum.
 = Here beginneth the Lamentation of Jeremias the Prophet. Aleph. How doth the city sit solitary that was full of people! how is the mistress of the Gentiles become as a widow: the princes of provinces made tributary! Beth. Weeping she hath wept in the night, and her tears are on her cheeks: there is none to comfort her among all them that were dear to her: all her friends have despised her, and are become her enemies. Ghimel. Juda hath removed her dwelling place because of her affliction, and the greatness of her bondage: she hath dwelt among the nations, and she hath found no rest: all her persecutors have taken her in the midst of straits. Jerusalem, Jerusalem, return to the Lord thy God.
F/
L: Responsorium I
 = Responsory 1
T: {℟.} In monte Olivéti orávit ad Patrem: Pater, si fíeri potest, tránseat a me calix iste: Spíritus quidem promptus est, caro autem infírma: * Fiat volúntas tua. {℣.} Verúmtamen non sicut ego volo, sed sicut tu vis. * Fiat.
 = {℟.} On the Mount of Olives he prayed to the Father: Father, if it be possible, let this chalice pass from me: the spirit indeed is willing, but the flesh is weak: * Thy will be done. {℣.} Nevertheless not as I will, but as thou wilt. * Thy will.
G: op-abl-r01 | In monte Olivéti
L: Lectio II
 = Lesson 2
I: Lam. 1, 4-6
 = Lamentations 1:4-6
G: op-ten-h-l02 | Lamentatio
F: Textus et versio | Text and translation
T: ^Daleth. Viæ Sion lugent eo quod non sint qui véniant ad solemnitátem: omnes portæ ejus destrúctæ: sacerdótes ejus geméntes: vírgines ejus squálidæ, et ipsa oppréssa amaritúdine. He. Facti sunt hostes ejus in cápite, inimíci ejus locupletáti sunt: quia Dóminus locútus est super eam propter multitúdinem iniquitátum ejus: párvuli ejus ducti sunt in captivitátem, ante fáciem tribulántis. Vau. Et egréssus est a fília Sion omnis decor ejus: facti sunt príncipes ejus velut aríetes non inveniéntes páscua: et abiérunt absque fortitúdine ante fáciem subsequéntis. Jerúsalem, Jerúsalem, convértere ad Dóminum Deum tuum.
 = Daleth. The ways of Sion mourn, because there are none that come to the solemn feast: all her gates are broken down: her priests sigh: her virgins are in affliction, and she is oppressed with bitterness. He. Her adversaries are become her lords, her enemies are enriched: because the Lord hath spoken against her for the multitude of her iniquities: her children are led into captivity: before the face of the oppressor. Vau. And from the daughter of Sion all her beauty is departed: her princes are become like rams that find no pastures: and they are gone away without strength before the face of the pursuer. Jerusalem, Jerusalem, return to the Lord thy God.
F/
L: Responsorium II
 = Responsory 2
T: {℟.} Tristis est ánima mea usque ad mortem: sustinéte hic, et vigiláte mecum: nunc vidébitis turbam, quæ circúmdabit me: * Vos fugam capiétis, et ego vadam immolári pro vobis. {℣.} Ecce appropínquat hora, et Fílius hóminis tradétur in manus peccatórum. * Vos fugam.
 = {℟.} My soul is sorrowful even unto death: stay here and watch with me: now you shall see the crowd that will surround me: * You will take flight, and I shall go to be sacrificed for you. {℣.} Behold the hour is at hand, and the Son of man shall be betrayed into the hands of sinners. * You will take flight.
G: op-abl-r02 | Tristis est ánima mea
L: Lectio III
 = Lesson 3
I: Lam. 1, 7-9
 = Lamentations 1:7-9
G: op-ten-h-l03 | Lamentatio
F: Textus et versio | Text and translation
T: ^Zain. Recordáta est Jerúsalem diérum afflictiónis suæ, et prævaricatiónis ómnium desiderabílium suórum, quæ habúerat a diébus antíquis, cum cáderet pópulus ejus in manu hostíli, et non esset auxiliátor: vidérunt eam hostes, et derisérunt sábbata ejus. Heth. Peccátum peccávit Jerúsalem, proptérea instábilis facta est: omnes, qui glorificábant eam, sprevérunt illam, quia vidérunt ignomíniam ejus: ipsa autem gemens convérsa est retrórsum. Teth. Sordes ejus in pédibus ejus, nec recordáta est finis sui: depósita est veheménter, non habens consolatórem: vide, Dómine, afflictiónem meam, quóniam eréctus est inimícus. Jerúsalem, Jerúsalem, convértere ad Dóminum Deum tuum.
 = Zain. Jerusalem hath remembered the days of her affliction, and prevarication of all her desirable things which she had from the days of old, when her people fell in the enemy's hand, and there was no helper: the enemies have seen her, and have mocked at her sabbaths. Heth. Jerusalem hath grievously sinned, therefore is she become unstable: all that honoured her have despised her, because they have seen her shame: but she sighed and turned backward. Teth. Her filthiness is on her feet, and she hath not remembered her end: she is wonderfully cast down, not having a comforter: behold, O Lord, my affliction, because the enemy is lifted up. Jerusalem, Jerusalem, return to the Lord thy God.
F/
L: Responsorium III
 = Responsory 3
T: {℟.} Ecce vídimus eum non habéntem spéciem, neque decórem: aspéctus ejus in eo non est. Hic peccáta nostra portávit, et pro nobis dolet: ipse autem vulnerátus est propter iniquitátes nostras: * Cujus livóre sanáti sumus. {℣.} Vere languóres nostros ipse tulit, et dolóres nostros ipse portávit. * Cujus.
 = {℟.} Behold, we have seen him without form or comeliness: there is no beauty in him. He has borne our sins and grieves for us; but he was wounded for our iniquities: * By whose bruises we are healed. {℣.} Surely he hath borne our infirmities, and carried our sorrows. * By whose bruises.
G: op-abl-r03 | Ecce vídimus eum
R: Repetitur Responsorium a principio usque ad Versum.
 = The Responsory is repeated from the beginning as far as the Verse.
H: IN II NOCTURNO
 = SECOND NOCTURN
T: {Aña} Liberávit {Ps. 71} Deus, judícium tuum.
 = {Ant.} The Lord… {Ps. 71}
F: Psalmus 71 Deus, judícium tuum | Psalm 71
G: op-ten-h-ps-71 | Tonus VII d
F: Textus et versio | Text and translation
T: Deus, iudícium tuum regi da: * et iustítiam tuam fílio regis:
 = Give to the king thy judgment, O God: * and to the king’s son thy justice:
T: Iudicáre pópulum tuum in iustítia, * et páuperes tuos in iudício.
 = To judge thy people with justice, * and thy poor with judgment.
T: Suscípiant montes pacem pópulo: * et colles iustítiam.
 = Let the mountains receive peace for the people: * and the hills justice.
T: Iudicábit páuperes pópuli, et salvos fáciet fílios páuperum: * et humiliábit calumniatórem.
 = He shall judge the poor of the people, and he shall save the children of the poor: * and he shall humble the oppressor.
T: Et permanébit cum sole, et ante lunam, * in generatióne et generatiónem.
 = And he shall continue with the sun, and before the moon, * throughout all generations.
T: Descéndet sicut plúvia in vellus: * et sicut stillicídia stillántia super terram.
 = He shall come down like rain upon the fleece; * and as showers falling gently upon the earth.
T: Oriétur in diébus eius iustítia, et abundántia pacis: * donec auferátur luna.
 = In his days shall justice spring up, and abundance of peace, * till the moon be taken away.
T: Et dominábitur a mari usque ad mare: * et a flúmine usque ad términos orbis terrárum.
 = And he shall rule from sea to sea, * and from the river unto the ends of the earth.
T: Coram illo prócident Æthíopes: * et inimíci eius terram lingent.
 = Before him the Ethiopians shall fall down: * and his enemies shall lick the ground.
T: Reges Tharsis, et ínsulæ múnera ófferent: * reges Árabum et Saba dona addúcent.
 = The kings of Tharsis and the islands shall offer presents: * the kings of the Arabians and of Saba shall bring gifts:
T: Et adorábunt eum omnes reges terræ: * omnes gentes sérvient ei:
 = And all kings of the earth shall adore him: * all nations shall serve him.
T: Quia liberábit páuperem a poténte: * et páuperem, cui non erat adiútor.
 = For he shall deliver the poor from the mighty: * and the needy that had no helper.
T: Parcet páuperi et ínopi: * et ánimas páuperum salvas fáciet.
 = He shall spare the poor and needy: * and he shall save the souls of the poor.
T: Ex usúris et iniquitáte rédimet ánimas eórum: * et honorábile nomen eórum coram illo.
 = He shall redeem their souls from usuries and iniquity: * and their names shall be honourable in his sight.
T: Et vivet, et dábitur ei de auro Arábiæ, et adorábunt de ipso semper: * tota die benedícent ei.
 = And he shall live, and to him shall be given of the gold of Arabia, for him they shall always adore: * they shall bless him all the day.
T: Et erit firmaméntum in terra in summis móntium, superextollétur super Líbanum fructus eius: * et florébunt de civitáte sicut fænum terræ.
 = And there shall be a firmament on the earth on the tops of mountains, above Libanus shall the fruit thereof be exalted: * and they of the city shall flourish like the grass of the earth.
T: Sit nomen eius benedíctum in sǽcula: * ante solem pérmanet nomen eius.
 = Let his name be blessed for evermore: * his name continueth before the sun.
T: Et benedicéntur in ipso omnes tribus terræ: * omnes gentes magnificábunt eum.
 = And in him shall all the tribes of the earth be blessed: * all nations shall magnify him.
T: Benedíctus Dóminus, Deus Israël, * qui facit mirabília solus:
 = Blessed be the Lord, the God of Israel, * who alone doth wonderful things.
T: Et benedíctum nomen maiestátis eius in ætérnum: * et replébitur maiestáte eius omnis terra: fiat, fiat.
 = And blessed be the name of his majesty for ever: * and the whole earth shall be filled with his majesty. So be it. So be it.
F/
F/
T: {Aña} Liberávit Dóminus páuperem a poténte: et ínopem, cui non erat adjútor.
 = {Ant.} The Lord hath delivered the poor from the mighty, and the needy that had no helper.
G: op-ten-h-t04 | Antiphona 4 · VII
T: {Aña} Cogitavérunt {Ps. 72} Quam bonus Israël Deus.
 = {Ant.} The wicked… {Ps. 72}
F: Psalmus 72 Quam bonus Israël Deus | Psalm 72
G: op-ten-h-ps-72 | Tonus VIII c
F: Textus et versio | Text and translation
T: Quam bonus Israël Deus, * his, qui recto sunt corde!
 = How good is God to Israel, * to them that are of a right heart!
T: Mei autem pæne moti sunt pedes: * pæne effúsi sunt gressus mei.
 = But my feet were almost moved; * my steps had wellnigh slipped.
T: Quia zelávi super iníquos, * pacem peccatórum videns.
 = Because I had a zeal on occasion of the wicked, * seeing the prosperity of sinners.
T: Quia non est respéctus morti eórum: * et firmaméntum in plaga eórum.
 = For there is no regard to their death, * nor is there strength in their stripes.
T: In labóre hóminum non sunt, * et cum homínibus non flagellabúntur:
 = They are not in the labour of men: * neither shall they be scourged like other men.
T: Ídeo ténuit eos supérbia, * opérti sunt iniquitáte et impietáte sua.
 = Therefore pride hath held them fast: * they are covered with their iniquity and their wickedness.
T: Pródiit quasi ex ádipe iníquitas eórum: * transiérunt in afféctum cordis.
 = Their iniquity hath come forth, as it were from fatness: * they have passed into the affection of the heart.
T: Cogitavérunt, et locúti sunt nequítiam: * iniquitátem in excélso locúti sunt.
 = They have thought and spoken wickedness: * they have spoken iniquity on high.
T: Posuérunt in cælum os suum: * et lingua eórum transívit in terra.
 = They have set their mouth against heaven: * and their tongue hath passed through the earth.
T: Ídeo convertétur pópulus meus hic: * et dies pleni inveniéntur in eis.
 = Therefore will my people return here * and full days shall be found in them.
T: Et dixérunt: Quómodo scit Deus, * et si est sciéntia in excélso?
 = And they said: How doth God know? * and is there knowledge in the most High?
T: Ecce, ipsi peccatóres, et abundántes in sǽculo, * obtinuérunt divítias.
 = Behold these are sinners; and yet abounding in the world * they have obtained riches.
T: Et dixi: Ergo sine causa iustificávi cor meum, * et lavi inter innocéntes manus meas:
 = And I said: Then have I in vain justified my heart, * and washed my hands among the innocent.
T: Et fui flagellátus tota die, * et castigátio mea in matutínis.
 = And I have been scourged all the day; * and my chastisement hath been in the mornings.
T: Si dicébam: Narrábo sic: * ecce, natiónem filiórum tuórum reprobávi.
 = If I said: I will speak thus; * behold I should condemn the generation of thy children.
T: Existimábam ut cognóscerem hoc, * labor est ante me:
 = I studied that I might know this thing, * it is a labour in my sight:
T: Donec intrem in Sanctuárium Dei: * et intéllegam in novíssimis eórum.
 = Until I go into the sanctuary of God, * and understand concerning their last ends.
T: Verúmtamen propter dolos posuísti eis: * deiecísti eos dum allevaréntur.
 = But indeed for deceits thou hast put it to them: * when they were lifted up, thou hast cast them down.
T: Quómodo facti sunt in desolatiónem, súbito defecérunt: * periérunt propter iniquitátem suam.
 = How are they brought to desolation? they have suddenly ceased to be: * they have perished by reason of their iniquity.
T: Velut sómnium surgéntium, Dómine, * in civitáte tua imáginem ipsórum ad níhilum rédiges.
 = As the dream of them that awake, O Lord; * so in thy city thou shalt bring their image to nothing.
T: Quia inflammátum est cor meum, et renes mei commutáti sunt: * et ego ad níhilum redáctus sum, et nescívi.
 = For my heart hath been inflamed, and my reins have been changed: * and I am brought to nothing, and I knew not.
T: Ut iuméntum factus sum apud te: * et ego semper tecum.
 = I am become as a beast before thee: * and I am always with thee.
T: Tenuísti manum déxteram meam: et in voluntáte tua deduxísti me, * et cum glória suscepísti me.
 = Thou hast held me by my right hand; and by thy will thou hast conducted me, * and with thy glory thou hast received me.
T: Quid enim mihi est in cælo? * et a te quid vólui super terram?
 = For what have I in heaven? * and besides thee what do I desire upon earth?
T: Defécit caro mea, et cor meum: * Deus cordis mei, et pars mea Deus in ætérnum.
 = For thee my flesh and my heart hath fainted away: * thou art the God of my heart, and the God that is my portion for ever.
T: Quia ecce, qui elóngant se a te, períbunt: * perdidísti omnes, qui fornicántur abs te.
 = For behold they that go far from thee shall perish: * thou hast destroyed all them that are disloyal to thee.
T: Mihi autem adhærére Deo bonum est: * pónere in Dómino Deo spem meam:
 = But it is good for me to adhere to my God, * to put my hope in the Lord God:
T: Ut annúntiem omnes prædicatiónes tuas, * in portis fíliæ Sion.
 = That I may declare all thy praises, * in the gates of the daughter of Sion.
F/
F/
T: {Aña} Cogitavérunt ímpii, et locúti sunt nequítiam: iniquitátem in excélso locúti sunt.
 = {Ant.} The wicked have thought and spoken wickedness: they have spoken iniquity on high.
G: op-ten-h-t05 | Antiphona 5 · VIII
T: {Aña} Exsúrge {Ps. 73} Ut quid, Deus.
 = {Ant.} Arise, O… {Ps. 73}
F: Psalmus 73 Ut quid, Deus | Psalm 73
G: op-ten-h-ps-73 | Tonus I a
F: Textus et versio | Text and translation
T: Ut quid, Deus, repulísti in finem: * irátus est furor tuus super oves páscuæ tuæ?
 = O God, why hast thou cast us off unto the end: * why is thy wrath enkindled against the sheep of thy pasture?
T: Memor esto congregatiónis tuæ, * quam possedísti ab inítio.
 = Remember thy congregation, * which thou hast possessed from the beginning.
T: Redemísti virgam hereditátis tuæ: * mons Sion, in quo habitásti in eo.
 = The sceptre of thy inheritance which thou hast redeemed: * mount Sion in which thou hast dwelt.
T: Leva manus tuas in supérbias eórum in finem: * quanta malignátus est inimícus in sancto!
 = Lift up thy hands against their pride unto the end; * see what things the enemy hath done wickedly in the sanctuary.
T: Et gloriáti sunt qui odérunt te: * in médio solemnitátis tuæ.
 = And they that hate thee have made their boasts, * in the midst of thy solemnity.
T: Posuérunt signa sua, signa: * et non cognovérunt sicut in éxitu super summum.
 = They have set up their ensigns for signs, * and they knew not both in the going out and on the highest top.
T: Quasi in silva lignórum secúribus excidérunt iánuas eius in idípsum: * in secúri et áscia deiecérunt eam.
 = As with axes in a wood of trees, they have cut down at once the gates thereof, * with axe and hatchet they have brought it down.
T: Incendérunt igni Sanctuárium tuum: * in terra polluérunt tabernáculum nóminis tui.
 = They have set fire to thy sanctuary: * they have defiled the dwelling place of thy name on the earth.
T: Dixérunt in corde suo cognátio eórum simul: * Quiéscere faciámus omnes dies festos Dei a terra.
 = They said in their heart, the whole kindred of them together: * Let us abolish all the festival days of God from the land.
T: Signa nostra non vídimus, iam non est prophéta: * et nos non cognóscet ámplius.
 = Our signs we have not seen, there is now no prophet: * and he will know us no more.
T: Úsquequo, Deus, improperábit inimícus: * irrítat adversárius nomen tuum in finem?
 = How long, O God, shall the enemy reproach: * is the adversary to provoke thy name for ever?
T: Ut quid avértis manum tuam, et déxteram tuam, * de médio sinu tuo in finem?
 = Why dost thou turn away thy hand: and thy right hand * out of the midst of thy bosom for ever?
T: Deus autem Rex noster ante sǽcula: * operátus est salútem in médio terræ.
 = But God is our king before ages: * he hath wrought salvation in the midst of the earth.
T: Tu confirmásti in virtúte tua mare: * contribulásti cápita dracónum in aquis.
 = Thou by thy strength didst make the sea firm: * thou didst crush the heads of the dragons in the waters.
T: Tu confregísti cápita dracónis: * dedísti eum escam pópulis Æthíopum.
 = Thou hast broken the heads of the dragon: * thou hast given him to be meat for the people of the Ethiopians.
T: Tu dirupísti fontes, et torréntes: * tu siccásti flúvios Ethan.
 = Thou hast broken up the fountains and the torrents: * thou hast dried up the Ethan rivers.
T: Tuus est dies, et tua est nox: * tu fabricátus es auróram et solem.
 = Thine is the day, and thine is the night: * thou hast made the morning light and the sun.
T: Tu fecísti omnes términos terræ: * æstátem et ver tu plasmásti ea.
 = Thou hast made all the borders of the earth: * the summer and the spring were formed by thee.
T: Memor esto huius, inimícus improperávit Dómino: * et pópulus insípiens incitávit nomen tuum.
 = Remember this, the enemy hath reproached the Lord: * and a foolish people hath provoked thy name.
T: Ne tradas béstiis ánimas confiténtes tibi, * et ánimas páuperum tuórum ne obliviscáris in finem.
 = Deliver not up to beasts the souls that confess to thee: * and forget not to the end the souls of thy poor.
T: Réspice in testaméntum tuum: * quia repléti sunt, qui obscuráti sunt terræ dómibus iniquitátum.
 = Have regard to thy covenant: * for they that are the obscure of the earth have been filled with dwellings of iniquity.
T: Ne avertátur húmilis factus confúsus: * pauper et inops laudábunt nomen tuum.
 = Let not the humble be turned away with confusion: * the poor and needy shall praise thy name.
T: Exsúrge, Deus, iúdica causam tuam: * memor esto improperiórum tuórum, eórum quæ ab insipiénte sunt tota die.
 = Arise, O God, judge thy own cause: * remember thy reproaches with which the foolish man hath reproached thee all the day.
T: Ne obliviscáris voces inimicórum tuórum: * supérbia eórum, qui te odérunt, ascéndit semper.
 = Forget not the voices of thy enemies: * the pride of them that hate thee ascendeth continually.
F/
F/
T: {Aña} Exsúrge, Dómine, et júdica causam meam.
 = {Ant.} Arise, O Lord, and judge my cause.
G: op-ten-h-t06 | Antiphona 6 · I
G: op-ten-h-v02 | Versus
T: {℣.} Deus meus, éripe me de manu peccatóris. {℟.} Et de manu contra legem agéntis et iníqui.
 = {℣.} Deliver me, O my God, out of the hand of the sinner. {℟.} And out of the hand of the transgressor of the law and of the unjust.
R: Pater noster, secreto.
 = The Our Father, silently.
L: Lectio IV
 = Lesson 4
I: Sermo sancti Leónis Papæ (Serm. 7 de Passione)
 = Sermon of St Leo the Pope (7th on the Passion)
T: ^Incipiéntes, dilectíssimi, de Passióne Dómini tractáre, divíno intellígimus dispósitum fuísse consílio, ut sacrílegi Judæórum príncipes, et ímpii sacerdótes, qui sæviéndi in Christum occasiónes sæpe quæsíerant: nonnísi in solemnitáte Pascháli exercéndi furóris sui accíperent potestátem. Oportébat enim ut manifésto impleréntur efféctu, quæ diu fúerant figuráto promíssa mystério: et uno explerétur sacrifício variárum differéntia victimárum. Ut ergo umbræ céderent córpori, et cessárent imágines sub præséntia veritátis: antíqua observántia novo exclúditur sacraménto. Hóstia in hóstiam transit, et sánguine sanguis aufértur: et legális festívitas, dum mutátur, implétur.
 = Beginning, dearly beloved, to treat of the Passion of the Lord, we understand that it was ordained by the divine counsel that the sacrilegious chiefs of the Jews and the impious priests, who had often sought occasions of venting their rage on Christ, should receive power to exercise their fury only at the solemnity of the Pasch. For it was fitting that what had long been promised in a figurative mystery should be fulfilled in plain effect, and that the diversity of victims should be completed by one sacrifice. That the shadows therefore might give way to the body, and the images cease in the presence of the truth, the ancient observance is set aside by a new sacrament. Victim passes into victim, blood takes away blood, and the feast of the Law, while it is changed, is fulfilled.
L: Responsorium IV
 = Responsory 4
T: {℟.} Amícus meus ósculi me trádidit signo. Quem osculátus fúero, ipse est, tenéte eum. Hoc malum fecit signum, qui per ósculum adimplévit homicídium. * Infélix prætermísit prétium sánguinis, et in fine láqueo se suspéndit. {℣.} Fílius quidem hóminis vadit, sicut scriptum est de illo: væ autem hómini illi, per quem tradétur. * Infélix.
 = {℟.} My friend betrayed me by the sign of a kiss: Whom I shall kiss, that is he: hold him fast. He who committed murder by a kiss gave this wicked sign. * The unhappy man cast away the price of blood, and in the end hanged himself. {℣.} The Son of man indeed goes, as it is written of him; but woe to that man by whom he shall be betrayed. * The unhappy man.
G: op-abl-r04 | Amícus meus
L: Lectio V
 = Lesson 5
T: ^Incipiénte enim festivitáte Paschali, qui ornáre templum, vasa mundáre, víctimas providére debúerant, in unum fácinus conjúrant: ut et nova mystéria non apprehénderent, et antíqua violárent. Providéntibus ergo ne in die sancto tumúltus orirétur: non festivitáti, sed facínori studebátur. Metuebántque, non ut pópulus non peccáret: sed ne Christus eváderet. At Jesus consílii sui certus, vetus testaméntum consummábat: et novum Pascha condébat. Et cum in Cáiphæ átrio tractarétur, quómodo Christus debéret occídi: ille Córporis et Sánguinis sui órdinans sacraméntum, docébat qualis Deo hóstia debéret offérri, nec traditóre submóto: ut ostenderétur nulla injúria exasperátus.
 = For when the paschal feast was beginning, those who should have been adorning the temple, cleansing the vessels and providing the victims, conspire together in one crime: so that they neither grasped the new mysteries and violated the old. Taking care, then, that no tumult should arise on the holy day, they were zealous not for the feast but for the crime; and they feared, not lest the people should sin, but lest Christ should escape. But Jesus, sure of his own counsel, was fulfilling the Old Testament and founding the new Pasch. And while in the court of Caiphas they were discussing how Christ should be put to death, he, ordaining the sacrament of his Body and Blood, was teaching what kind of victim ought to be offered to God; nor was the traitor put away, so that it might be shown that he was not angered by any injury.
L: Responsorium V
 = Responsory 5
T: {℟.} Unus ex vobis tradet me hódie: væ illi, per quem tradar ego: * Mélius illi erat, si natus non fuísset. {℣.} Qui intíngit mecum manum in parópside, hic me traditúrus est in manus peccatórum. * Mélius.
 = {℟.} One of you will betray me today: woe to him by whom I shall be betrayed: * It were better for him if he had not been born. {℣.} He that dippeth his hand with me in the dish, he will betray me into the hands of sinners. * It were better.
G: op-abl-r05 | Unus ex vobis
L: Lectio VI
 = Lesson 6
T: ^Dicens quoque, Unus vestrum me traditúrus est, notam sibi traditóris ac proditóris consciéntiam demonstrávit: non áspera, nec apérta ímpium increpatióne confúndens, sed leni ac tácita admonitióne convéniens: ut facílius corríperet pœnitúdo, quem nulla deformásset abjéctio. Cur, infélix Juda, tanta benignitáte non úteris? Ecce parcit áusibus tuis Dóminus, et nulli te, nisi tibi, índicat Christus: nec nomen tuum, nec persóna detégitur: sed veritátis et misericórdiæ verbo, cordis tui tantum arcána tangúntur. Non Apostólici órdinis honor, non sacramentórum tibi commúnio denegátur. Redi in íntegrum, et depósito furóre resipísce: cleméntia invítat, salus pulsat: ad vitam te Vita révocat.
 = Saying also, One of you is about to betray me, he showed the traitor and betrayer that his conscience was known: not confounding the wicked one with harsh or open rebuke, but meeting him with a gentle and silent warning, so that repentance might the more easily correct one whom no rejection had disgraced. Why, unhappy Judas, dost thou not make use of such great kindness? Behold, the Lord spares thy daring, and Christ points thee out to no one but thyself; neither thy name nor thy person is uncovered, but only the secrets of thy heart are touched by the word of truth and mercy. The honour of the apostolic order is not denied thee, nor the communion of the sacraments. Return to thy right mind, and lay aside thy fury and come to thy senses: mercy invites, salvation knocks, Life calls thee back to life.
L: Responsorium VI
 = Responsory 6
T: {℟.} Eram quasi agnus ínnocens: ductus sum ad immolándum, et nesciébam: consílium fecérunt inimíci mei advérsum me, dicéntes: * Veníte, mittámus lignum in panem ejus, et eradámus eum de terra vivéntium. {℣.} Omnes inimíci mei advérsum me cogitábant mala mihi: verbum iníquum mandavérunt advérsum me, dicéntes. * Veníte.
 = {℟.} I was like an innocent lamb: I was led to be sacrificed, and I knew it not: my enemies took counsel against me, saying: * Come, let us put wood into his bread, and cut him off from the land of the living. {℣.} All my enemies devised evils against me: they uttered a wicked word against me, saying. * Come.
G: op-abl-r06 | Eram quasi agnus
R: Repetitur Responsorium a principio usque ad Versum.
 = The Responsory is repeated from the beginning as far as the Verse.
H: IN III NOCTURNO
 = THIRD NOCTURN
T: {Aña} Dixi {Ps. 74} Confitébimur tibi.
 = {Ant.} I said… {Ps. 74}
F: Psalmus 74 Confitébimur tibi | Psalm 74
G: op-ten-h-ps-74 | Tonus VII d
F: Textus et versio | Text and translation
T: Confitébimur tibi, Deus: * confitébimur, et invocábimus nomen tuum.
 = We will praise thee, O God: * we will praise, and we will call upon thy name.
T: Narrábimus mirabília tua: * cum accépero tempus, ego iustítias iudicábo.
 = We will relate thy wondrous works: * when I shall take a time, I will judge justices.
T: Liquefácta est terra, et omnes qui hábitant in ea: * ego confirmávi colúmnas eius.
 = The earth is melted, and all that dwell therein: * I have established the pillars thereof.
T: Dixi iníquis: Nolíte iníque ágere: * et delinquéntibus: Nolíte exaltáre cornu:
 = I said to the wicked: Do not act wickedly: * and to the sinners: Lift not up the horn.
T: Nolíte extóllere in altum cornu vestrum: * nolíte loqui advérsus Deum iniquitátem.
 = Lift not up your horn on high: * speak not iniquity against God.
T: Quia neque ab Oriénte, neque ab Occidénte, neque a desértis móntibus: * quóniam Deus iudex est.
 = For neither from the east, nor from the west, nor from the desert hills: * for God is the judge.
T: Hunc humíliat, et hunc exáltat: * quia calix in manu Dómini vini meri plenus misto.
 = One he putteth down, and another he lifteth up: * for in the hand of the Lord there is a cup of strong wine full of mixture.
T: Et inclinávit ex hoc in hoc: verúmtamen fæx eius non est exinaníta: * bibent omnes peccatóres terræ.
 = And he hath poured it out from this to that: but the dregs thereof are not emptied: * all the sinners of the earth shall drink.
T: Ego autem annuntiábo in sǽculum: * cantábo Deo Iacob.
 = But I will declare for ever: * I will sing to the God of Jacob.
T: Et ómnia córnua peccatórum confríngam: * et exaltabúntur córnua iusti.
 = And I will break all the horns of sinners: * but the horns of the just shall be exalted.
F/
F/
T: {Aña} Dixi iníquis: Nolíte loqui advérsus Deum iniquitátem.
 = {Ant.} I said to the wicked: Speak not iniquity against God.
G: op-ten-h-t07 | Antiphona 7 · VII
T: {Aña} Terra {Ps. 75} Notus in Judǽa.
 = {Ant.} The earth… {Ps. 75}
F: Psalmus 75 Notus in Judǽa | Psalm 75
G: op-ten-h-ps-75 | Tonus VIII c
F: Textus et versio | Text and translation
T: Notus in Iudǽa Deus: * in Israël magnum nomen eius.
 = In Judea God is known: * his name is great in Israel.
T: Et factus est in pace locus eius: * et habitátio eius in Sion.
 = And his place is in peace: * and his abode in Sion:
T: Ibi confrégit poténtias árcuum, * scutum, gládium, et bellum.
 = There hath he broken the powers of bows, * the shield, the sword, and the battle.
T: Illúminans tu mirabíliter a móntibus ætérnis: * turbáti sunt omnes insipiéntes corde.
 = Thou enlightenest wonderfully from the everlasting hills. * All the foolish of heart were troubled.
T: Dormiérunt somnum suum: * et nihil invenérunt omnes viri divitiárum in mánibus suis.
 = They have slept their sleep; * and all the men of riches have found nothing in their hands.
T: Ab increpatióne tua, Deus Iacob, * dormitavérunt qui ascendérunt equos.
 = At thy rebuke, O God of Jacob, * they have all slumbered that mounted on horseback.
T: Tu terríbilis es, et quis resístet tibi? * ex tunc ira tua.
 = Thou art terrible, and who shall resist thee? * from that time thy wrath.
T: De cælo audítum fecísti iudícium: * terra trémuit et quiévit,
 = Thou hast caused judgment to be heard from heaven: * the earth trembled and was still,
T: Cum exsúrgeret in iudícium Deus, * ut salvos fáceret omnes mansuétos terræ.
 = When God arose in judgment, * to save all the meek of the earth.
T: Quóniam cogitátio hóminis confitébitur tibi: * et relíquiæ cogitatiónis diem festum agent tibi.
 = For the thought of man shall give praise to thee: * and the remainders of the thought shall keep holiday to thee.
T: Vovéte, et réddite Dómino, Deo vestro: * omnes, qui in circúitu eius affértis múnera.
 = Vow ye, and pay to the Lord your God: * all you that are round about him bring presents.
T: Terríbili et ei qui aufert spíritum príncipum, * terríbili apud reges terræ.
 = To him that is terrible, even to him who taketh away the spirit of princes: * to the terrible with the kings of the earth.
F/
F/
T: {Aña} Terra trémuit et quiévit, dum resúrgeret in judícium Deus.
 = {Ant.} The earth trembled and was still, when God arose in judgement.
G: op-ten-h-t08 | Antiphona 8 · VIII
T: {Aña} In {Ps. 76} Voce mea ad Dóminum.
 = {Ant.} In the… {Ps. 76}
F: Psalmus 76 Voce mea ad Dóminum | Psalm 76
G: op-ten-h-ps-76 | Tonus VII a
F: Textus et versio | Text and translation
T: Voce mea ad Dóminum clamávi: * voce mea ad Deum, et inténdit mihi.
 = I cried to the Lord with my voice; * to God with my voice, and he gave ear to me.
T: In die tribulatiónis meæ Deum exquisívi, mánibus meis nocte contra eum: * et non sum decéptus.
 = In the day of my trouble I sought God, with my hands lifted up to him in the night, * and I was not deceived.
T: Rénuit consolári ánima mea, * memor fui Dei, et delectátus sum, et exercitátus sum: et defécit spíritus meus.
 = My soul refused to be comforted: * I remembered God, and was delighted, and was exercised, and my spirit swooned away.
T: Anticipavérunt vigílias óculi mei: * turbátus sum, et non sum locútus.
 = My eyes prevented the watches: * I was troubled, and I spoke not.
T: Cogitávi dies antíquos: * et annos ætérnos in mente hábui.
 = I thought upon the days of old: * and I had in my mind the eternal years.
T: Et meditátus sum nocte cum corde meo, * et exercitábar, et scopébam spíritum meum.
 = And I meditated in the night with my own heart: * and I was exercised and I swept my spirit.
T: Numquid in ætérnum proíciet Deus: * aut non appónet ut complacítior sit adhuc?
 = Will God then cast off for ever? * or will he never be more favourable again?
T: Aut in finem misericórdiam suam abscíndet, * a generatióne in generatiónem?
 = Or will he cut off his mercy for ever, * from generation to generation?
T: Aut obliviscétur miseréri Deus? * aut continébit in ira sua misericórdias suas?
 = Or will God forget to shew mercy? * or will he in his anger shut up his mercies?
T: Et dixi: Nunc cœpi: * hæc mutátio déxteræ Excélsi.
 = And I said, Now have I begun: * this is the change of the right hand of the most High.
T: Memor fui óperum Dómini: * quia memor ero ab inítio mirabílium tuórum.
 = I remembered the works of the Lord: * for I will be mindful of thy wonders from the beginning.
T: Et meditábor in ómnibus opéribus tuis: * et in adinventiónibus tuis exercébor.
 = And I will meditate on all thy works: * and will be employed in thy inventions.
T: Deus, in sancto via tua: quis Deus magnus sicut Deus noster? * tu es Deus qui facis mirabília.
 = Thy way, O God, is in the holy place: who is the great God like our God? * Thou art the God that dost wonders.
T: Notam fecísti in pópulis virtútem tuam: * redemísti in brácchio tuo pópulum tuum, fílios Iacob et Ioseph.
 = Thou hast made thy power known among the nations: * with thy arm thou hast redeemed thy people, the children of Jacob and of Joseph.
T: Vidérunt te aquæ, Deus, vidérunt te aquæ: * et timuérunt, et turbátæ sunt abýssi.
 = The waters saw thee, O God, the waters saw thee: * and they were afraid, and the depths were troubled.
T: Multitúdo sónitus aquárum: * vocem dedérunt nubes.
 = Great was the noise of the waters: * the clouds sent out a sound.
T: Étenim sagíttæ tuæ tránseunt: * vox tonítrui tui in rota.
 = For thy arrows pass: * the voice of thy thunder in a wheel.
T: Illuxérunt coruscatiónes tuæ orbi terræ: * commóta est, et contrémuit terra.
 = Thy lightnings enlightened the world: * the earth shook and trembled.
T: In mari via tua, et sémitæ tuæ in aquis multis: * et vestígia tua non cognoscéntur.
 = Thy way is in the sea, and thy paths in many waters: * and thy footsteps shall not be known.
T: Deduxísti sicut oves pópulum tuum, * in manu Móysi et Aaron.
 = Thou hast conducted thy people like sheep, * by the hand of Moses and Aaron.
F/
F/
T: {Aña} In die tribulatiónis meæ Deum exquisívi mánibus meis.
 = {Ant.} In the day of my trouble I sought God with my hands.
G: op-ten-h-t09 | Antiphona 9 · VII
G: op-ten-h-v03 | Versus
T: {℣.} Homo pacis meæ, in quo sperávi. {℟.} Ampliávit advérsum me supplantatiónem.
 = {℣.} The man of my peace, in whom I trusted. {℟.} Hath greatly supplanted me.
R: Pater noster, secreto.
 = The Our Father, silently.
L: Lectio VII
 = Lesson 7
I: Sermo sancti Joánnis Chrysóstomi (Hom. de proditione Judæ)
 = Sermon of St John Chrysostom (Homily on the betrayal by Judas)
T: ^Tunc ábiit, inquit. Non invitátus a princípibus: sponte procéssit. Unus de duódecim. Septuagínta discípulis non tam interióra commíssa sunt. Hæc erat catérva regális. De hoc choro Judas egréssus est. Judas, inquit, Iscariótes. Quid mihi ejus pátriam dicis? Utinam ipsum quoque nescíre licuísset. Et dixit eis: Quid vultis mihi dare, et ego eum vobis tradam? O puniéndæ vocis temeritátem. Ideo Magíster te divítias debére contémnere prædicábat? O crudelitátem. Dic quæ causa proditiónis. Fórsitan quod tibi dæmonum trádidit potestátem, quod fecit languéntibus subveníre: quod mórtuos suscitáre didicísti. O verbum totíus supérbiæ. Tradis eum, qui cuncta consérvat, qui dæmónibus ímperat, cui obtémperant mária: cui omnes súbjacent potestátes.
 = Then he went, it says. He was not invited by the chief priests; he went of his own accord. One of the twelve. Things so intimate were not entrusted to the seventy disciples. This was the royal band; from this choir Judas went out. Judas, it says, Iscariot. Why dost thou tell me his country? Would that it had been possible not to know him either. And he said to them: What will you give me, and I will deliver him unto you? O the rashness of a word deserving punishment! Was it for this that the Master preached that thou shouldst despise riches? O cruelty! Tell me the cause of the betrayal. Perhaps that he gave thee power over demons, that he enabled thee to help the sick, that thou didst learn to raise the dead? O word of utter pride! Thou betrayest him who preserves all things, who commands the demons, whom the seas obey, to whom all powers are subject.
L: Responsorium VII
 = Responsory 7
T: {℟.} Una hora non potuístis vigiláre mecum, qui exhortabámini mori pro me? * Vel Judam non vidétis, quómodo non dormit, sed festínat trádere me Judǽis? {℣.} Dormíte jam, et requiéscite: ecce appropínquat, qui me tradet. * Vel Judam.
 = {℟.} Could you not watch one hour with me, you who urged one another to die for me? * Or do you not see Judas, how he sleeps not, but hastens to betray me to the Jews? {℣.} Sleep now and take your rest: behold, he is at hand that will betray me. * Or do you not see Judas.
G: op-abl-r07 | Una hora
L: Lectio VIII
 = Lesson 8
T: ^Quando Judas de traditióne tractábat, dicunt discípuli: Ubi vis parémus tibi Pascha manducáre? Vide discípulos: vide discípulum. Isti sunt de præparatióne sollíciti: ille Domínici sánguinis mercátor exsístit. Et illud quidem Pascha Judæórum discípuli præparábant: istud autem, quod nostrum est, ipse Christus constítuit. Et dixit: Hoc est Corpus meum, quod pro vobis tradétur. Hic est Sanguis meus, qui pro multis effundétur. O Christi misericórdia. O Judæ deméntia. Ille eum paciscebátur, ut vénderet: Christus ei sánguinem, quem véndidit, offerébat, ut habéret remissiónem peccatórum: si tamen ímpius exsístere noluísset. Nam sacrifícii communicatiónem méruit, et pedes ejus lavit Christus: ut excusatiónem suæ malítiæ non habéret.
 = While Judas was plotting the betrayal, the disciples say: Where wilt thou that we prepare for thee to eat the Pasch? Look at the disciples, look at the disciple. These are anxious about the preparation; he stands forth as the merchant of the Lord's blood. And that Pasch of the Jews the disciples were preparing; but this, which is ours, Christ himself instituted. And he said: This is my Body, which shall be given for you. This is my Blood, which shall be shed for many. O the mercy of Christ! O the madness of Judas! He was bargaining to sell him; Christ was offering him the blood that he sold, that he might have remission of sins, if only he had not chosen to be wicked. For he was found worthy of communion in the sacrifice, and Christ washed his feet, that he might have no excuse for his malice.
L: Responsorium VIII
 = Responsory 8
T: {℟.} Senióres pópuli consílium fecérunt, * Ut Jesum dolo tenérent, et occíderent: cum gládiis, et fústibus exiérunt tamquam ad latrónem. {℣.} Collegérunt ergo Pontífices et Pharisǽi concílium. * Ut Jesum.
 = {℟.} The elders of the people took counsel, * That they might take Jesus by guile and put him to death: they went out as against a robber, with swords and clubs. {℣.} The chief priests and the Pharisees therefore gathered a council. * That they might take.
G: op-abl-r08 | Senióres pópuli
L: Lectio IX
 = Lesson 9
T: ^Sed jam ad terríbilem mensam cum débita reveréntia, cum cóngrua vigilántia, opportúnum tempus invítat. Nullus ibi Judas reperiátur, nullus malígnus accédat: nec insídiæ mente condántur. Nullus fictus accédat: nullus fucáto ánimo. Pura ígitur sit mens, pura cogitátio: quia et sacrifícium purum est. Dóminus quidem noster, corpus assúmptum ablatúrus ex óculis nostris, et sidéribus illatúrus, in hac die sacraméntum Córporis et Sánguinis sui consecrávit: ut perénnis illa víctima víveret in memória, et semper præsens esset in grátia. Cum ígitur ad reveréndum altáre ascéndis: sacrum Dei tui Corpus et Sánguinem fide réspice, honóre miráre, mente cóntinge: cordis manu súscipe, et gustu interióri assúme.
 = But now the fitting time invites us to the awesome table with due reverence and becoming watchfulness. Let no Judas be found there, let no evil one approach, nor let treachery be hidden in the mind. Let no one approach in pretence, no one with a painted soul. Let the mind then be pure, the thought pure, for the sacrifice too is pure. Our Lord, being about to take the body he had assumed out of our sight and to carry it to the stars, on this day consecrated the sacrament of his Body and Blood, that that everlasting victim might live in memory and be always present in grace. When therefore thou goest up to the venerable altar, look with faith upon the sacred Body and Blood of thy God, wonder at it with honour, touch it with the mind, receive it with the hand of the heart, and take it with the inward taste.
L: Responsorium IX
 = Responsory 9
T: {℟.} Revelábunt cæli iniquitátem Judæ, et terra advérsus eum consúrget: et maniféstum erit peccátum illíus in die furóris Dómini: * Cum eis, qui dixérunt Dómino Deo: Recéde a nobis, sciéntiam viárum tuárum nólumus. {℣.} In diem perditiónis servábitur, et ad diem ultiónis ducétur. * Cum eis.
 = {℟.} The heavens shall reveal the iniquity of Judas, and the earth shall rise up against him: and his sin shall be made manifest in the day of the Lord's wrath: * With those who said to the Lord God: Depart from us, we desire not the knowledge of thy ways. {℣.} He shall be kept for the day of destruction, and shall be led to the day of vengeance. * With those.
G: op-abl-r09 | Revelábunt cæli
R: Repetitur Responsorium a principio usque ad Versum.
 = The Responsory is repeated from the beginning as far as the Verse.
R: Hoc triduo, finito IX ℟., non dicatur ℣. ante Laudes, sed statim inchoëtur in Laudibus Antiphona prima.
 = On these three days, when the ninth Responsory is finished, no Versicle is said before Lauds, but the first Antiphon of Lauds is begun at once.
== Ad Laudes | Lauds
H: AD LAUDES
 = LAUDS
S: Ecclesiasticum Officium O.P. Triduo ante Pascha (1927), pp. 31-44
T: {Aña} Justificéris. {Psalmus 50}
 = {Ant.} That thou mayest be justified… {Psalm 50}
F: Psalmus 50 Miserére | Psalm 50
G: op-ten-h-ps-50 | Tonus VIII G
F: Textus et versio | Text and translation
T: Miserére mei, Deus, * secúndum magnam misericórdiam tuam.
 = Have mercy on me, O God, * according to thy great mercy.
T: Et secúndum multitúdinem miseratiónum tuárum, * dele iniquitátem meam.
 = And according to the multitude of thy tender mercies * blot out my iniquity.
T: Ámplius lava me ab iniquitáte mea: * et a peccáto meo munda me.
 = Wash me yet more from my iniquity, * and cleanse me from my sin.
T: Quóniam iniquitátem meam ego cognósco: * et peccátum meum contra me est semper.
 = For I know my iniquity, * and my sin is always before me.
T: Tibi soli peccávi, et malum coram te feci: * ut iustificéris in sermónibus tuis, et vincas cum iudicáris.
 = To thee only have I sinned, and have done evil before thee: * that thou mayst be justified in thy words, and mayst overcome when thou art judged.
T: Ecce enim, in iniquitátibus concéptus sum: * et in peccátis concépit me mater mea.
 = For behold I was conceived in iniquities; * and in sins did my mother conceive me.
T: Ecce enim, veritátem dilexísti: * incérta et occúlta sapiéntiæ tuæ manifestásti mihi.
 = For behold thou hast loved truth: * the uncertain and hidden things of thy wisdom thou hast made manifest to me.
T: Aspérges me hyssópo, et mundábor: * lavábis me, et super nivem dealbábor.
 = Thou shalt sprinkle me with hyssop, and I shall be cleansed: * thou shalt wash me, and I shall be made whiter than snow.
T: Audítui meo dabis gáudium et lætítiam: * et exsultábunt ossa humiliáta.
 = To my hearing thou shalt give joy and gladness: * and the bones that have been humbled shall rejoice.
T: Avérte fáciem tuam a peccátis meis: * et omnes iniquitátes meas dele.
 = Turn away thy face from my sins, * and blot out all my iniquities.
T: Cor mundum crea in me, Deus: * et spíritum rectum ínnova in viscéribus meis.
 = Create a clean heart in me, O God: * and renew a right spirit within my bowels.
T: Ne proícias me a fácie tua: * et spíritum sanctum tuum ne áuferas a me.
 = Cast me not away from thy face; * and take not thy holy spirit from me.
T: Redde mihi lætítiam salutáris tui: * et spíritu principáli confírma me.
 = Restore unto me the joy of thy salvation, * and strengthen me with a perfect spirit.
T: Docébo iníquos vias tuas: * et ímpii ad te converténtur.
 = I will teach the unjust thy ways: * and the wicked shall be converted to thee.
T: Líbera me de sanguínibus, Deus, Deus salútis meæ: * et exsultábit lingua mea iustítiam tuam.
 = Deliver me from blood, O God, thou God of my salvation: * and my tongue shall extol thy justice.
T: Dómine, lábia mea apéries: * et os meum annuntiábit laudem tuam.
 = O Lord, thou wilt open my lips: * and my mouth shall declare thy praise.
T: Quóniam si voluísses sacrifícium, dedíssem útique: * holocáustis non delectáberis.
 = For if thou hadst desired sacrifice, I would indeed have given it: * with burnt offerings thou wilt not be delighted.
T: Sacrifícium Deo spíritus contribulátus: * cor contrítum, et humiliátum, Deus, non despícies.
 = A sacrifice to God is an afflicted spirit: * a contrite and humbled heart, O God, thou wilt not despise.
T: Benígne fac, Dómine, in bona voluntáte tua Sion: * ut ædificéntur muri Ierúsalem.
 = Deal favourably, O Lord, in thy good will with Sion; * that the walls of Jerusalem may be built up.
T: Tunc acceptábis sacrifícium iustítiæ, oblatiónes, et holocáusta: * tunc impónent super altáre tuum vítulos.
 = Then shalt thou accept the sacrifice of justice, oblations and whole burnt offerings: * then shall they lay calves upon thy altar.
F/
F/
T: {Aña} Justificéris, Dómine, in sermónibus tuis, et vincas cum judicáris.
 = {Ant.} That thou mayest be justified, O Lord, in thy words, and mayest overcome when thou art judged.
G: op-ten-h-u01 | Antiphona 1 · VIII
T: {Aña} Dóminus. {Psalmus 89}
 = {Ant.} The Lord was led as a sheep to the slaughter… {Psalm 89}
F: Psalmus 89 Dómine, refúgium | Psalm 89
G: op-ten-h-ps-89 | Tonus II D
F: Textus et versio | Text and translation
T: Dómine, refúgium factus es nobis: * a generatióne in generatiónem.
 = Lord, thou hast been our refuge * from generation to generation.
T: Priúsquam montes fíerent, aut formarétur terra et orbis: * a sǽculo et usque in sǽculum tu es, Deus.
 = Before the mountains were made, or the earth and the world was formed; * from eternity and to eternity thou art God.
T: Ne avértas hóminem in humilitátem: * et dixísti: Convertímini, fílii hóminum.
 = Turn not man away to be brought low: * and thou hast said: Be converted, O ye sons of men.
T: Quóniam mille anni ante óculos tuos, * tamquam dies hestérna, quæ prætériit,
 = For a thousand years in thy sight * are as yesterday, which is past.
T: Et custódia in nocte, * quæ pro níhilo habéntur, eórum anni erunt.
 = And as a watch in the night, * things that are counted nothing, shall their years be.
T: Mane sicut herba tránseat, mane flóreat, et tránseat: * véspere décidat, indúret et aréscat.
 = In the morning man shall grow up like grass; in the morning he shall flourish and pass away: * in the evening he shall fall, grow dry, and wither.
T: Quia defécimus in ira tua, * et in furóre tuo turbáti sumus.
 = For in thy wrath we have fainted away: * and are troubled in thy indignation.
T: Posuísti iniquitátes nostras in conspéctu tuo: * sǽculum nostrum in illuminatióne vultus tui.
 = Thou hast set our iniquities before thy eyes: * our life in the light of thy countenance.
T: Quóniam omnes dies nostri defecérunt: * et in ira tua defécimus.
 = For all our days are spent; * and in thy wrath we have fainted away.
T: Anni nostri sicut aránea meditabúntur: * dies annórum nostrórum in ipsis, septuagínta anni.
 = Our years shall be considered as a spider: * the days of our years in them are threescore and ten years.
T: Si autem in potentátibus, octogínta anni: * et ámplius eórum, labor et dolor.
 = But if in the strong they be fourscore years: * and what is more of them is labour and sorrow.
T: Quóniam supervénit mansuetúdo: * et corripiémur.
 = For mildness is come upon us: * and we shall be corrected.
T: Quis novit potestátem iræ tuæ: * et præ timóre tuo iram tuam dinumeráre?
 = Who knoweth the power of thy anger, * and for thy fear can number thy wrath?
T: Déxteram tuam sic notam fac: * et erudítos corde in sapiéntia.
 = So make thy right hand known: * and men learned in heart, in wisdom.
T: Convértere, Dómine, úsquequo? * et deprecábilis esto super servos tuos.
 = Return, O Lord, how long? * and be entreated in favour of thy servants.
T: Repléti sumus mane misericórdia tua: * et exsultávimus, et delectáti sumus ómnibus diébus nostris.
 = We are filled in the morning with thy mercy: * and we have rejoiced, and are delighted all our days.
T: Lætáti sumus pro diébus, quibus nos humiliásti: * annis, quibus vídimus mala.
 = We have rejoiced for the days in which thou hast humbled us: * for the years in which we have seen evils.
T: Réspice in servos tuos, et in ópera tua: * et dírige fílios eórum.
 = Look upon thy servants and upon their works: * and direct their children.
T: Et sit splendor Dómini, Dei nostri, super nos, et ópera mánuum nostrárum dírige super nos: * et opus mánuum nostrárum dírige.
 = And let the brightness of the Lord our God be upon us: and direct thou the works of our hands over us; * yea, the work of our hands do thou direct.
F/
F/
T: {Aña} Dóminus tamquam ovis ad víctimam ductus est, et non apéruit os suum.
 = {Ant.} The Lord was led as a sheep to the slaughter, and he opened not his mouth.
G: op-ten-h-u02 | Antiphona 2 · II
T: {Aña} Contrítum est. {Psalmus 62}
 = {Ant.} My heart is broken within me; all my bones tremble.… {Psalm 62}
F: Psalmus 62 Deus, Deus meus | Psalm 62
G: op-ten-h-ps-62 | Tonus VIII G
F: Textus et versio | Text and translation
T: Deus, Deus meus, * ad te de luce vígilo.
 = O God, my God, * to thee do I watch at break of day.
T: Sitívit in te ánima mea, * quam multiplíciter tibi caro mea.
 = For thee my soul hath thirsted; * for thee my flesh, O how many ways!
T: In terra desérta, et ínvia, et inaquósa: * sic in sancto appárui tibi, ut vidérem virtútem tuam, et glóriam tuam.
 = In a desert land, and where there is no way, and no water: * so in the sanctuary have I come before thee, to see thy power and thy glory.
T: Quóniam mélior est misericórdia tua super vitas: * lábia mea laudábunt te.
 = For thy mercy is better than lives: * thee my lips shall praise.
T: Sic benedícam te in vita mea: * et in nómine tuo levábo manus meas.
 = Thus will I bless thee all my life long: * and in thy name I will lift up my hands.
T: Sicut ádipe et pinguédine repleátur ánima mea: * et lábiis exsultatiónis laudábit os meum.
 = Let my soul be filled as with marrow and fatness: * and my mouth shall praise thee with joyful lips.
T: Si memor fui tui super stratum meum, in matutínis meditábor in te: * quia fuísti adiútor meus.
 = If I have remembered thee upon my bed, I will meditate on thee in the morning: * because thou hast been my helper.
T: Et in velaménto alárum tuárum exsultábo, adhǽsit ánima mea post te: * me suscépit déxtera tua.
 = And I will rejoice under the covert of thy wings: my soul hath stuck close to thee: * thy right hand hath received me.
T: Ipsi vero in vanum quæsiérunt ánimam meam, introíbunt in inferióra terræ: * tradéntur in manus gládii, partes vúlpium erunt.
 = But they have sought my soul in vain, they shall go into the lower parts of the earth: * they shall be delivered into the hands of the sword, they shall be the portions of foxes.
T: Rex vero lætábitur in Deo, laudabúntur omnes qui iurant in eo: * quia obstrúctum est os loquéntium iníqua.
 = But the king shall rejoice in God, all they shall be praised that swear by him: * because the mouth is stopped of them that speak wicked things.
F/
F/
T: {Aña} Contrítum est cor meum in médio mei: contremuérunt ómnia ossa mea.
 = {Ant.} My heart is broken within me; all my bones tremble.
G: op-ten-h-u03 | Antiphona 3 · VIII
T: {Aña} Exhortátus es. {Cant. Moysis}
 = {Ant.} Thou hast encouraged us in thy strength… {Canticle of Moses (Exod. 15)}
F: Canticum Moysis (Exod. 15) Cantémus Dómino | Canticle of Moses (Exod. 15)
G: op-ten-h-ps-moysis | Tonus IV A A
F: Textus et versio | Text and translation
T: Cantémus Dómino: glorióse enim magnificátus est, * equum et ascensórem deiécit in mare.
 = Let us sing to the Lord: for he is gloriously magnified, * the horse and the rider he hath thrown into the sea.
T: Fortitúdo mea, et laus mea Dóminus, * et factus est mihi in salútem.
 = The Lord is my strength and my praise, * and he is become salvation to me:
T: Iste Deus meus, et glorificábo eum: * Deus patris mei, et exaltábo eum.
 = He is my God and I will glorify him: * the God of my father, and I will exalt him.
T: Dóminus quasi vir pugnátor, Omnípotens nomen eius. * Currus Pharaónis et exércitum eius proiécit in mare.
 = The Lord is as a man of war, Almighty is his name. * Pharao’s chariots and his army he hath cast into the sea:
T: Elécti príncipes eius submérsi sunt in Mari Rubro: * abýssi operuérunt eos, descendérunt in profúndum quasi lapis.
 = His chosen captains are drowned in the Red Sea. * The depths have covered them, they are sunk to the bottom like a stone.
T: Déxtera tua, Dómine, magnificáta est in fortitúdine: déxtera tua, Dómine, percússit inimícum. * Et in multitúdine glóriæ tuæ deposuísti adversários tuos:
 = Thy right hand, O Lord, is magnified in strength: thy right hand, O Lord, hath slain the enemy. * And in the multitude of thy glory thou hast put down thy adversaries:
T: Misísti iram tuam, quæ devorávit eos sicut stípulam. * Et in spíritu furóris tui congregátæ sunt aquæ:
 = Thou hast sent thy wrath, which hath devoured them like stubble. * And with the blast of thy anger the waters were gathered together:
T: Stetit unda fluens, * congregátæ sunt abýssi in médio mari.
 = The flowing water stood, * the depths were gathered together in the midst of the sea.
T: Dixit inimícus: Pérsequar et comprehéndam, * dívidam spólia, implébitur ánima mea:
 = The enemy said: I will pursue and overtake, * I will divide the spoils, my soul shall have its fill:
T: Evaginábo gládium meum, * interfíciet eos manus mea.
 = I will draw my sword, * my hand shall slay them.
T: Flavit spíritus tuus, et opéruit eos mare: * submérsi sunt quasi plumbum in aquis veheméntibus.
 = Thy wind blew and the sea covered them: * they sunk as lead in the mighty waters.
T: Quis símilis tui in fórtibus, Dómine? * quis símilis tui, magníficus in sanctitáte, terríbilis atque laudábilis, fáciens mirabília?
 = Who is like to thee, among the strong, O Lord? * who is like to thee, glorious in holiness, terrible and praiseworthy, doing wonders?
T: Extendísti manum tuam, et devorávit eos terra. * Dux fuísti in misericórdia tua pópulo quem redemísti:
 = Thou stretchedst forth thy hand, and the earth swallowed them. * In thy mercy thou hast been a leader to the people which thou hast redeemed:
T: Et portásti eum in fortitúdine tua, * ad habitáculum sanctum tuum.
 = And in thy strength thou hast carried them * to thy holy habitation.
T: Ascendérunt pópuli, et iráti sunt: * dolóres obtinuérunt habitatóres Philísthiim.
 = Nations rose up, and were angry: * sorrows took hold on the inhabitants of Philisthiim.
T: Tunc conturbáti sunt príncipes Edom, robústos Moab obtínuit tremor: * obriguérunt omnes habitatóres Chánaan.
 = Then were the princes of Edom troubled, trembling seized on the stout men of Moab: * all the inhabitants of Chanaan became stiff.
T: Írruat super eos formído et pavor, * in magnitúdine brácchii tui:
 = Let fear and dread fall upon them, * in the greatness of thy arm:
T: Fiant immóbiles quasi lapis, donec pertránseat pópulus tuus, Dómine, * donec pertránseat pópulus tuus iste, quem possedísti.
 = Let them become unmoveable as a stone, until thy people, O Lord, pass by: * until this thy people pass by, which thou hast possessed.
T: Introdúces eos, et plantábis in monte hereditátis tuæ, * firmíssimo habitáculo tuo quod operátus es, Dómine:
 = Thou shalt bring them in, and plant them in the mountain of thy inheritance, * in thy most firm habitation which thou hast made, O Lord;
T: Sanctuárium tuum, Dómine, quod firmavérunt manus tuæ. * Dóminus regnábit in ætérnum et ultra.
 = Thy sanctuary, O Lord, which thy hands have established. * The Lord shall reign for ever and ever.
T: Ingréssus est enim eques Phárao cum cúrribus et equítibus eius in mare: * et redúxit super eos Dóminus aquas maris:
 = For Pharao went in on horseback with his chariots and horsemen into the sea: * and the Lord brought back upon them the waters of the sea:
T: Fílii autem Israël ambulavérunt per siccum * in médio eius.
 = But the children of Israel walked on dry ground * in the midst thereof.
F/
F/
T: {Aña} Exhortátus es in virtúte tua, et in refectióne sancta tua, Dómine.
 = {Ant.} Thou hast encouraged us in thy strength, and in thy holy refreshment, O Lord.
G: op-ten-h-u04 | Antiphona 4 · IV
T: {Aña} Oblátus est. {Psalmus 148}
 = {Ant.} He was offered because it was his own will… {Psalm 148}
F: Psalmus 148 Laudáte Dóminum de cælis | Psalm 148
G: op-ten-h-ps-148 | Tonus II D
F: Textus et versio | Text and translation
T: Laudáte Dóminum de cælis: * laudáte eum in excélsis.
 = Praise ye the Lord from the heavens * praise ye him in the high places.
T: Laudáte eum, omnes Ángeli eius: * laudáte eum, omnes virtútes eius.
 = Praise ye him, all his angels: * praise ye him, all his hosts.
T: Laudáte eum, sol et luna: * laudáte eum, omnes stellæ et lumen.
 = Praise ye him, O sun and moon: * praise him, all ye stars and light.
T: Laudáte eum, cæli cælórum: * et aquæ omnes, quæ super cælos sunt, laudent nomen Dómini.
 = Praise him, ye heavens of heavens: * and let all the waters that are above the heavens, praise the name of the Lord.
T: Quia ipse dixit, et facta sunt: * ipse mandávit, et creáta sunt.
 = For he spoke, and they were made: * he commanded, and they were created.
T: Státuit ea in ætérnum, et in sǽculum sǽculi: * præcéptum pósuit, et non præteríbit.
 = He hath established them for ever, and for ages of ages: * he hath made a decree, and it shall not pass away.
T: Laudáte Dóminum de terra, * dracónes, et omnes abýssi.
 = Praise the Lord from the earth, * ye dragons, and all ye deeps:
T: Ignis, grando, nix, glácies, spíritus procellárum: * quæ fáciunt verbum eius:
 = Fire, hail, snow, ice, stormy winds * which fulfill his word:
T: Montes, et omnes colles: * ligna fructífera, et omnes cedri.
 = Mountains and all hills, * fruitful trees and all cedars:
T: Béstiæ, et univérsa pécora: * serpéntes, et vólucres pennátæ:
 = Beasts and all cattle: * serpents and feathered fowls:
T: Reges terræ, et omnes pópuli: * príncipes, et omnes iúdices terræ.
 = Kings of the earth and all people: * princes and all judges of the earth:
T: Iúvenes, et vírgines: senes cum iunióribus laudent nomen Dómini: * quia exaltátum est nomen eius solíus.
 = Young men and maidens: let the old with the younger, praise the name of the Lord: * For his name alone is exalted.
T: Conféssio eius super cælum et terram: * et exaltávit cornu pópuli sui.
 = The praise of him is above heaven and earth: * and he hath exalted the horn of his people.
T: Hymnus ómnibus sanctis eius: * fíliis Israël, pópulo appropinquánti sibi.
 = A hymn to all his saints: to the children of Israel, a people approaching him.
F/
F/
T: {Aña} Oblátus est, quia ipse vóluit: et peccáta nostra ipse portávit.
 = {Ant.} He was offered because it was his own will, and he bore our sins.
G: op-ten-h-u05 | Antiphona 5 · II
R: Capitulum non dicatur, neque Hymnus, neque Versiculus; sed dum Psalmus *Laudate* dicitur, lumen, ubi videri nequeat, abscondatur; et lampades, quæ sunt per Ecclesiam, exstinguantur. Finita quinta Antiphona post *Laudate,* mox Antiphona ad *Benedictus,* ab eo, qui præest, incipiatur; et cereus, qui usque tunc solus ardens remansit, extinguatur, sicque *Benedictus,* et omnia, quæ sequuntur, in tenebris dicantur.
 = The Chapter, Hymn and Versicle are not said; but while the Psalm *Laudate* is said, the light is hidden where it cannot be seen, and the lamps throughout the church are put out. When the fifth Antiphon after *Laudate* is finished, the one presiding at once begins the Antiphon at the *Benedictus,* and the candle that alone has remained burning until then is put out; and so the *Benedictus* and all that follows are said in darkness.
L: Ad Benedictus
 = At the Benedictus
T: {Aña} Tráditor. {Cant. Zachariæ} Benedíctus Dóminus Deus Israël.
 = {Ant.} The traitor… {Canticle of Zachary}
F: Canticum Zachariæ (Luc. 1) Benedíctus | Canticle of Zachary (Luke 1)
G: op-ten-h-ps-zachariae | Tonus I g
F: Textus et versio | Text and translation
T: Benedíctus ✠ Dóminus, Deus Israël: * quia visitávit, et fecit redemptiónem plebis suæ:
 = Blessed be the Lord ✠ God of Israel; * because he hath visited and wrought the redemption of his people:
T: Et eréxit cornu salútis nobis: * in domo David, púeri sui.
 = And hath raised up an horn of salvation to us, * in the house of David his servant:
T: Sicut locútus est per os sanctórum, * qui a sǽculo sunt, prophetárum eius:
 = As he spoke by the mouth of his holy Prophets, * who are from the beginning:
T: Salútem ex inimícis nostris, * et de manu ómnium, qui odérunt nos.
 = Salvation from our enemies, * and from the hand of all that hate us:
T: Ad faciéndam misericórdiam cum pátribus nostris: * et memorári testaménti sui sancti.
 = To perform mercy to our fathers, * and to remember his holy testament,
T: Iusiurándum, quod iurávit ad Ábraham patrem nostrum, * datúrum se nobis:
 = The oath, which he swore to Abraham our father, * that he would grant to us,
T: Ut sine timóre, de manu inimicórum nostrórum liberáti, * serviámus illi.
 = That being delivered from the hand of our enemies, * we may serve him without fear,
T: In sanctitáte, et iustítia coram ipso, * ómnibus diébus nostris.
 = In holiness and justice before him, * all our days.
T: Et tu, puer, Prophéta Altíssimi vocáberis: * præíbis enim ante fáciem Dómini, paráre vias eius:
 = And thou, child, shalt be called the prophet of the Highest: * for thou shalt go before the face of the Lord to prepare his ways:
T: Ad dandam sciéntiam salútis plebi eius: * in remissiónem peccatórum eórum:
 = To give knowledge of salvation to his people, * unto the remission of their sins:
T: Per víscera misericórdiæ Dei nostri: * in quibus visitávit nos, óriens ex alto:
 = Through the bowels of the mercy of our God, * in which the Orient from on high hath visited us:
T: Illumináre his, qui in ténebris, et in umbra mortis sedent: * ad dirigéndos pedes nostros in viam pacis.
 = To enlighten them that sit in darkness, and in the shadow of death: * to direct our feet into the way of peace.
F/
F/
T: {Aña} Tráditor autem dedit eis signum, dicens: Quem osculátus fúero, ipse est, tenéte eum.
 = {Ant.} Now the traitor had given them a sign, saying: Whomsoever I shall kiss, that is he: hold him fast.
G: op-ten-h-u06 | Antiphona ad Benedictus · I
L: Kyrie
 = Kyrie
T: Kýrie, eléison. Dómine, miserére. Christus Dóminus factus est obédiens usque ad mortem.
 = Lord, have mercy. O Lord, have mercy. Christ the Lord became obedient unto death.
R: Finita Antiphona post *Benedictus,* duo Fratres stantes ante gradus Altaris cantent:
 = When the Antiphon after the *Benedictus* is finished, two brethren standing before the altar steps sing:
G: op-ten-h-k01 | Kyrie eleison
R: Et Choro respondente:
 = And the Choir answering:
G: op-ten-h-k01 | Kyrie eleison
R: Iterum illi duo Fratres cantent:
 = Again those two brethren sing:
G: op-ten-h-k01 | Kyrie eleison
R: Deinde alii duo Fratres stantes in medio Chori cantent:
 = Then two other brethren standing in the middle of the Choir sing:
G: op-ten-h-k04 | Domine miserere
R: Et Chorus respondeat:
 = And the Choir answers:
G: op-ten-h-k05 | Christus Dominus factus est
T: {℣.} Qui passúrus advenísti propter nos.
 = {℣.} Thou who camest to suffer for us.
R: Fratres ante gradus cantent:
 = The brethren before the steps sing:
G: op-ten-h-k06 | Qui passurus
R: Chorus:
 = Choir:
G: op-ten-h-k07 | Christe eleison
T: {℣.} Qui expánsis in cruce mánibus, traxísti ómnia ad te sǽcula.
 = {℣.} Thou who, with thy hands stretched out upon the Cross, didst draw all ages to thee.
R: Fratres ante gradus:
 = The brethren before the steps:
G: op-ten-h-k08 | Qui expansis
R: Chorus:
 = Choir:
G: op-ten-h-k07 | Christe eleison
T: {℣.} Qui prophétice prompsísti: Ero mors tua, o mors.
 = {℣.} Thou who didst say by the prophet: O death, I will be thy death.
R: Fratres ante gradus:
 = The brethren before the steps:
G: op-ten-h-k10 | Qui prophetice
R: Chorus:
 = Choir:
G: op-ten-h-k07 | Christe eleison
R: Deinde duo Fratres in medio Chori cantent:
 = Then two brethren in the middle of the Choir sing:
G: op-ten-h-k04 | Domine miserere
R: Et Chorus respondeat:
 = And the Choir answers:
G: op-ten-h-k05 | Christus Dominus factus est
R: Item Fratres ante gradus cantent:
 = Again the brethren before the steps sing:
G: op-ten-h-k01 | Kyrie eleison
R: Et Chorus respondeat:
 = And the Choir answers:
G: op-ten-h-k01 | Kyrie eleison
R: Et Fratres ante gradus:
 = And the brethren before the steps:
G: op-ten-h-k01 | Kyrie eleison
R: Deinde duo Fratres in medio Chori cantent:
 = Then two brethren in the middle of the Choir sing:
G: op-ten-h-k04 | Domine miserere
R: Et Chorus respondeat:
 = And the Choir answers:
G: op-ten-h-k05 | Christus Dominus factus est
R: Denique duo Fratres ante gradus alta voce cantent:
 = Lastly two brethren before the steps sing in a loud voice:
G: op-ten-h-k19 | Mortem autem crucis
R: Interim dum hæc cantantur, Fratres stent versi ad Altare.
 = Meanwhile, while these are sung, the brethren stand turned towards the altar.
R: Finito Versu *Mortem autem crucis,* prosternant se omnes super formas, et dicant *Pater noster,* secreto, et facto signo a Prælato dicant mediocri voce alternatim Psalmum 50 *Miserére mei Deus.*
 = When the Verse *Mortem autem crucis* is finished, all prostrate themselves on the forms and say the *Pater noster* silently, and at a sign from the Prelate they say Psalm 50 *Miserére mei Deus* alternately in a moderate voice.
F: Psalmus 50 Miserére | Psalm 50
T: Miserére mei, Deus, * secúndum magnam misericórdiam tuam.
 = Have mercy on me, O God, * according to thy great mercy.
T: Et secúndum multitúdinem miseratiónum tuárum, * dele iniquitátem meam.
 = And according to the multitude of thy tender mercies * blot out my iniquity.
T: Ámplius lava me ab iniquitáte mea: * et a peccáto meo munda me.
 = Wash me yet more from my iniquity, * and cleanse me from my sin.
T: Quóniam iniquitátem meam ego cognósco: * et peccátum meum contra me est semper.
 = For I know my iniquity, * and my sin is always before me.
T: Tibi soli peccávi, et malum coram te feci: * ut iustificéris in sermónibus tuis, et vincas cum iudicáris.
 = To thee only have I sinned, and have done evil before thee: * that thou mayst be justified in thy words, and mayst overcome when thou art judged.
T: Ecce enim, in iniquitátibus concéptus sum: * et in peccátis concépit me mater mea.
 = For behold I was conceived in iniquities; * and in sins did my mother conceive me.
T: Ecce enim, veritátem dilexísti: * incérta et occúlta sapiéntiæ tuæ manifestásti mihi.
 = For behold thou hast loved truth: * the uncertain and hidden things of thy wisdom thou hast made manifest to me.
T: Aspérges me hyssópo, et mundábor: * lavábis me, et super nivem dealbábor.
 = Thou shalt sprinkle me with hyssop, and I shall be cleansed: * thou shalt wash me, and I shall be made whiter than snow.
T: Audítui meo dabis gáudium et lætítiam: * et exsultábunt ossa humiliáta.
 = To my hearing thou shalt give joy and gladness: * and the bones that have been humbled shall rejoice.
T: Avérte fáciem tuam a peccátis meis: * et omnes iniquitátes meas dele.
 = Turn away thy face from my sins, * and blot out all my iniquities.
T: Cor mundum crea in me, Deus: * et spíritum rectum ínnova in viscéribus meis.
 = Create a clean heart in me, O God: * and renew a right spirit within my bowels.
T: Ne proícias me a fácie tua: * et spíritum sanctum tuum ne áuferas a me.
 = Cast me not away from thy face; * and take not thy holy spirit from me.
T: Redde mihi lætítiam salutáris tui: * et spíritu principáli confírma me.
 = Restore unto me the joy of thy salvation, * and strengthen me with a perfect spirit.
T: Docébo iníquos vias tuas: * et ímpii ad te converténtur.
 = I will teach the unjust thy ways: * and the wicked shall be converted to thee.
T: Líbera me de sanguínibus, Deus, Deus salútis meæ: * et exsultábit lingua mea iustítiam tuam.
 = Deliver me from blood, O God, thou God of my salvation: * and my tongue shall extol thy justice.
T: Dómine, lábia mea apéries: * et os meum annuntiábit laudem tuam.
 = O Lord, thou wilt open my lips: * and my mouth shall declare thy praise.
T: Quóniam si voluísses sacrifícium, dedíssem útique: * holocáustis non delectáberis.
 = For if thou hadst desired sacrifice, I would indeed have given it: * with burnt offerings thou wilt not be delighted.
T: Sacrifícium Deo spíritus contribulátus: * cor contrítum, et humiliátum, Deus, non despícies.
 = A sacrifice to God is an afflicted spirit: * a contrite and humbled heart, O God, thou wilt not despise.
T: Benígne fac, Dómine, in bona voluntáte tua Sion: * ut ædificéntur muri Ierúsalem.
 = Deal favourably, O Lord, in thy good will with Sion; * that the walls of Jerusalem may be built up.
T: Tunc acceptábis sacrifícium iustítiæ, oblatiónes, et holocáusta: * tunc impónent super altáre tuum vítulos.
 = Then shalt thou accept the sacrifice of justice, oblations and whole burnt offerings: * then shall they lay calves upon thy altar.
F/
R: Deinde, absque *Dóminus vobíscum,* et absque *Orémus,* dicatur:
 = Then, without *Dóminus vobíscum* and without *Orémus,* is said:
L: Oratio
 = Prayer
T: ^Réspice, quǽsumus, Dómine, super hanc famíliam tuam, pro qua Dóminus noster Jesus Christus non dubitávit mánibus tradi nocéntium, et crucis subíre torméntum.
 = Look down, we beseech thee, O Lord, upon this thy family, for which our Lord Jesus Christ did not hesitate to be delivered into the hands of the wicked, and to undergo the torment of the Cross.
R: Non dicatur *Qui tecum,* neque *Amen,* neque *Benedicámus Dómino,* neque *Fidélium,* sed dicto post Orationem *Pater noster,* facto signo a Prælato, surgant omnes, et lumen a Sacrista proferatur. Et sic hoc triduo Matutinum terminetur.
 = *Qui tecum,* *Amen,* *Benedicámus Dómino* and *Fidélium* are not said; but after the Prayer the *Pater noster* is said, and at a sign from the Prelate all rise, and the light is brought out by the Sacristan. And so Matins ends on these three days.
R: Hac die non fiant prostrationes ad Horas, propter solemnitatem Cenæ, nisi in hac Hora Laudum tantum.
 = On this day there are no prostrations at the Hours, because of the solemnity of the Supper, except at this Hour of Lauds only.
== Ad Horas | The Little Hours
S: Ecclesiasticum Officium O.P. Triduo ante Pascha (1927), pp. 44-56
R: Usque ad Completorium Sabbati exclusive, non dicatur *Deus, in adjutórium,* in principio Horarum; nec *Convérte nos,* ad Completorium; nec *Glória Patri,* in fine Psalmorum et Canticorum, nisi in Vesperis Sabbati; nec Capitulum, nec Hymnus, nec ℟., nec Versiculus, nec *Dóminus vobíscum,* nec *Orémus,* nec *Qui tecum vivit,* nec *Benedicámus Dómino,* nec *Fidélium,* post Horas. More tamen solito dicatur *Pater noster.*
 = Until Compline of Holy Saturday exclusive, *Deus, in adjutórium* is not said at the beginning of the Hours; nor *Convérte nos* at Compline; nor *Glória Patri* at the end of the Psalms and Canticles, except at Vespers of Holy Saturday; nor the Chapter, Hymn, Responsory or Versicle, nor *Dóminus vobíscum,* *Orémus,* *Qui tecum vivit,* *Benedicámus Dómino* or *Fidélium* after the Hours. But the *Pater noster* is said as usual.
R: Antiphonæ vero *Salve Regína,* et *Pie Pater Domínice,* cum Versiculis et Orationibus in terminatione Horarum dici solitis omittantur usque ad Pascha exclusive.
 = The antiphons *Salve Regína* and *Pie Pater Domínice,* with the Versicles and Prayers usually said at the end of the Hours, are left out until Easter exclusive.
H: AD PRIMAM
 = PRIME
R: Dicto *Pater noster,* et *Credo in Deum,* postquam versis vultibus ad Altare Fratres se signaverint, inchoëtur:
 = When the *Pater noster* and the *Credo* has been said, and the brethren, turned towards the altar, have signed themselves, there is begun:
T: {Aña} Christus factus est. {Ps. 53}
 = {Ant.} Christ became obedient… {Ps. 53}
F: Psalmus 53 Deus, in nómine tuo | Psalm 53
G: op-ten-h-ps-53 | Tonus VIII G
F: Textus et versio | Text and translation
T: Deus, in nómine tuo salvum me fac: * et in virtúte tua iúdica me.
 = Save me, O God, by thy name, * and judge me in thy strength.
T: Deus, exáudi oratiónem meam: * áuribus pércipe verba oris mei.
 = O God, hear my prayer: * give ear to the words of my mouth.
T: Quóniam aliéni insurrexérunt advérsum me, et fortes quæsiérunt ánimam meam: * et non proposuérunt Deum ante conspéctum suum.
 = For strangers have risen up against me; and the mighty have sought after my soul: * and they have not set God before their eyes.
T: Ecce enim, Deus ádiuvat me: * et Dóminus suscéptor est ánimæ meæ.
 = For behold God is my helper: * and the Lord is the protector of my soul.
T: Avérte mala inimícis meis: * et in veritáte tua dispérde illos.
 = Turn back the evils upon my enemies; * and cut them off in thy truth.
T: Voluntárie sacrificábo tibi, * et confitébor nómini tuo, Dómine: quóniam bonum est:
 = I will freely sacrifice to thee, * and will give praise, O God, to thy name: because it is good:
T: Quóniam ex omni tribulatióne eripuísti me: * et super inimícos meos despéxit óculus meus.
 = For thou hast delivered me out of all trouble: * and my eye hath looked down upon my enemies.
F/
F/
F: Psalmus 118, i Beáti immaculáti | Psalm 118, i
G: op-ten-h-ps-118-1-16 | Tonus VIII G
F: Textus et versio | Text and translation
T: Beáti immaculáti in via: * qui ámbulant in lege Dómini.
 = Blessed are the undefiled in the way, * who walk in the law of the Lord.
T: Beáti, qui scrutántur testimónia eius: * in toto corde exquírunt eum.
 = Blessed are they that search his testimonies: * that seek him with their whole heart.
T: Non enim qui operántur iniquitátem, * in viis eius ambulavérunt.
 = For they that work iniquity, * have not walked in his ways.
T: Tu mandásti * mandáta tua custodíri nimis.
 = Thou hast commanded * thy commandments to be kept most diligently.
T: Útinam dirigántur viæ meæ, * ad custodiéndas iustificatiónes tuas!
 = O! that my ways may be directed * to keep thy justifications.
T: Tunc non confúndar, * cum perspéxero in ómnibus mandátis tuis.
 = Then shall I not be confounded, * when I shall look into all thy commandments.
T: Confitébor tibi in directióne cordis: * in eo quod dídici iudícia iustítiæ tuæ.
 = I will praise thee with uprightness of heart, * when I shall have learned the judgments of thy justice.
T: Iustificatiónes tuas custódiam: * non me derelínquas usquequáque.
 = I will keep thy justifications: * O! do not thou utterly forsake me.
T: In quo córrigit adolescéntior viam suam? * In custodiéndo sermónes tuos.
 = By what doth a young man correct his way? * By observing thy words.
T: In toto corde meo exquisívi te: * ne repéllas me a mandátis tuis.
 = With my whole heart have I sought after thee: * let me not stray from thy commandments.
T: In corde meo abscóndi elóquia tua: * ut non peccem tibi.
 = Thy words have I hidden in my heart, * that I may not sin against thee.
T: Benedíctus es, Dómine: * doce me iustificatiónes tuas.
 = Blessed art thou, O Lord: * teach me thy justifications.
T: In lábiis meis, * pronuntiávi ómnia iudícia oris tui.
 = With my lips * I have pronounced all the judgments of thy mouth.
T: In via testimoniórum tuórum delectátus sum, * sicut in ómnibus divítiis.
 = I have been delighted in the way of thy testimonies, * as in all riches.
T: In mandátis tuis exercébor: * et considerábo vias tuas.
 = I will meditate on thy commandments: * and I will consider thy ways.
T: In iustificatiónibus tuis meditábor: * non oblivíscar sermónes tuos.
 = I will think of thy justifications: * I will not forget thy words.
F/
F/
F: Psalmus 118, ii Retríbue servo tuo | Psalm 118, ii
G: op-ten-h-ps-118-17-32 | Tonus VIII G
F: Textus et versio | Text and translation
T: Retríbue servo tuo, vivífica me: * et custódiam sermónes tuos:
 = Give bountifully to thy servant, enliven me * and I shall keep thy words.
T: Revéla óculos meos: * et considerábo mirabília de lege tua.
 = Open thou my eyes: * and I will consider the wondrous things of thy law.
T: Íncola ego sum in terra: * non abscóndas a me mandáta tua.
 = I am a sojourner on the earth: * hide not thy commandments from me.
T: Concupívit ánima mea desideráre iustificatiónes tuas, * in omni témpore.
 = My soul hath coveted to long for thy justifications, * at all times.
T: Increpásti supérbos: * maledícti qui declínant a mandátis tuis.
 = Thou hast rebuked the proud: * they are cursed who decline from thy commandments.
T: Aufer a me oppróbrium, et contémptum: * quia testimónia tua exquisívi.
 = Remove from me reproach and contempt: * because I have sought after thy testimonies.
T: Étenim sedérunt príncipes, et advérsum me loquebántur: * servus autem tuus exercebátur in iustificatiónibus tuis.
 = For princes sat, and spoke against me: * but thy servant was employed in thy justifications.
T: Nam et testimónia tua meditátio mea est: * et consílium meum iustificatiónes tuæ.
 = For thy testimonies are my meditation: * and thy justifications my counsel.
T: Adhǽsit paviménto ánima mea: * vivífica me secúndum verbum tuum.
 = My soul hath cleaved to the pavement: * quicken thou me according to thy word.
T: Vias meas enuntiávi, et exaudísti me: * doce me iustificatiónes tuas.
 = I have declared my ways, and thou hast heard me: * teach me thy justifications.
T: Viam iustificatiónum tuárum ínstrue me: * et exercébor in mirabílibus tuis.
 = Make me to understand the way of thy justifications: * and I shall be exercised in thy wondrous works.
T: Dormitávit ánima mea præ tǽdio: * confírma me in verbis tuis.
 = My soul hath slumbered through heaviness: * strengthen thou me in thy words.
T: Viam iniquitátis ámove a me: * et de lege tua miserére mei.
 = Remove from me the way of iniquity: * and out of thy law have mercy on me.
T: Viam veritátis elégi: * iudícia tua non sum oblítus.
 = I have chosen the way of truth: * thy judgments I have not forgotten.
T: Adhǽsi testimóniis tuis, Dómine: * noli me confúndere.
 = I have stuck to thy testimonies, O Lord: * put me not to shame.
T: Viam mandatórum tuórum cucúrri, * cum dilatásti cor meum.
 = I have run the way of thy commandments, * when thou didst enlarge my heart.
F/
F/
T: {Aña} Christus factus est pro nobis obédiens usque ad mortem, mortem autem crucis.
 = {Ant.} Christ became obedient for us unto death, even the death of the Cross.
G: op-ten-h-c01 | Antiphona · VIII
R: Dicta post Psalmos Antiphona, absque *Kýrie, eléison,* et Precibus, et sine Psalmo *Miserére,* dicatur Oratio:
 = When the Antiphon has been said after the Psalms, without *Kýrie, eléison,* the Preces and the Psalm *Miserére,* the Prayer is said:
L: Oratio
 = Prayer
T: ^Réspice, quǽsumus, Dómine, super hanc famíliam tuam, pro qua Dóminus noster Jesus Christus non dubitávit mánibus tradi nocéntium, et crucis subíre torméntum.
 = Look down, we beseech thee, O Lord, upon this thy family, for which our Lord Jesus Christ did not hesitate to be delivered into the hands of the wicked, and to undergo the torment of the Cross.
R: *Confíteor* non dicatur usque ad Completorium Sabbati exclusive.
 = The *Confíteor* is not said until Compline of Holy Saturday exclusive.
H: AD PRETIOSAM
 = AT THE PRETIOSA
R: Pronuntiatis Luna et quæ de Kalendario et Martyrologio pronuntianda sunt, et responso *Deo grátias,* dicatur *Pretiósa,* hoc modo:
 = When the Moon and what is to be announced from the Calendar and the Martyrology have been read, and *Deo grátias* answered, the *Pretiósa* is said in this way:
T: {℣.} Pretiósa est in conspéctu Dómini. {℟.} Mors sanctórum ejus.
 = {℣.} Precious in the sight of the Lord. {℟.} Is the death of his saints.
R: Oratio absque *Orémus*
 = The Prayer, without *Orémus*
T: ^Sancta María et omnes Sancti intercédant pro nobis ad Dóminum: ut mereámur adjuvári ab eo, qui vivit et regnat per ómnia sǽcula sæculórum. {℟.} Amen.
 = May holy Mary and all the Saints intercede for us with the Lord, that we may be worthy to be helped by him who lives and reigns for ever and ever. {℟.} Amen.
T: {℣.} Deus, in adjutórium meum inténde. {℟.} Dómine, ad adjuvándum me festína.
 = {℣.} O God, come to my assistance. {℟.} O Lord, make haste to help me.
R: Hic ℣. *Deus, in adjutórium,* cum ℟. *Dómine, ad adjuvándum,* ter dicatur. Deinde, omisso *Glória Patri,* subjungatur:
 = Here the ℣. *Deus, in adjutórium* with the ℟. *Dómine, ad adjuvándum* is said three times. Then, the *Glória Patri* being left out, is added:
T: Kýrie, eléison. Christe, eléison. Kýrie, eléison. Pater noster… {℣.} Et ne nos indúcas in tentatiónem. {℟.} Sed líbera nos a malo.
 = Lord, have mercy. Christ, have mercy. Lord, have mercy. Our Father… {℣.} And lead us not into temptation. {℟.} But deliver us from evil.
T: {℣.} Réspice, Dómine, in servos tuos, et in ópera tua, et dírige fílios eórum. {℟.} Et sit splendor Dómini Dei nostri super nos, et ópera mánuum nostrárum dírige super nos: et opus mánuum nostrárum dírige.
 = {℣.} Look upon thy servants, O Lord, and upon thy works, and direct their children. {℟.} And let the brightness of the Lord our God be upon us, and direct thou the works of our hands over us: yea, the work of our hands do thou direct.
R: Oratio absque *Orémus*
 = The Prayer, without *Orémus*
T: ^Dirígere et sanctificáre dignéris, Dómine sancte, Pater omnípotens, ætérne Deus, hódie corda et córpora nostra in lege tua, et in opéribus mandatórum tuórum: ut hic, et in ætérnum, te auxiliánte, semper salvi esse mereámur: Per Christum Dóminum nostrum. {℟.} Amen.
 = Vouchsafe, O holy Lord, almighty Father, eternal God, to direct and sanctify this day our hearts and bodies in thy law and in the works of thy commandments, that here and for ever, by thy help, we may be worthy to be always saved: Through Christ our Lord. {℟.} Amen.
T: Jube, domne, benedícere. {Benedictio} Divínum auxílium máneat semper nobíscum. {℟.} Amen.
 = Pray, father, a blessing. {Blessing} May the divine help remain with us always. {℟.} Amen.
L: Evangelium
 = Gospel
T: ^Secúndum Joánnem. Cap. 13. Ante diem festum Paschæ, sciens Jesus quia venit hora ejus ut tránseat ex hoc mundo ad Patrem: cum dilexísset suos, qui erant in mundo, in finem diléxit eos. Tu autem, Dómine, miserére nostri. {℟.} Deo grátias.
 = According to John, chapter 13. Before the festival day of the Pasch, Jesus, knowing that his hour was come that he should pass out of this world to the Father, having loved his own who were in the world, he loved them unto the end. But thou, O Lord, have mercy on us. {℟.} Thanks be to God.
R: Commemoratio Fratrum, Sororum, Familiarium, Benefactorum defunctorum Ordinis nostri.
 = Commemoration of the departed brethren, sisters, familiars and benefactors of our Order.
T: Requiéscant in pace. {℟.} Amen.
 = May they rest in peace. {℟.} Amen.
T: {Ps. 116} Laudáte Dóminum, omnes gentes: * laudáte eum, omnes pópuli. Quóniam confirmáta est super nos misericórdia ejus: * et véritas Dómini manet in ætérnum.
 = {Ps. 116} O praise the Lord, all ye nations: praise him, all ye people. For his mercy is confirmed upon us: and the truth of the Lord remaineth for ever.
R: Deinde, omisso *Glória Patri,* sequitur:
 = Then, the *Glória Patri* being left out, there follows:
T: {℣.} Osténde nobis, Dómine, misericórdiam tuam. {℟.} Et salutáre tuum da nobis. {℣.} Dóminus vobíscum. {℟.} Et cum spíritu tuo. Orémus.
 = {℣.} Show us, O Lord, thy mercy. {℟.} And grant us thy salvation. {℣.} The Lord be with you. {℟.} And with thy spirit. Let us pray.
L: Oratio
 = Prayer
T: ^Actiónes nostras, quǽsumus, Dómine, aspirándo prǽveni et adjuvándo proséquere: ut cuncta nostra operátio a te semper incípiat, et per te cœpta finiátur. Per Christum Dóminum nostrum. {℟.} Amen.
 = Go before our actions, we beseech thee, O Lord, by thy inspiration, and further them by thy help, that every work of ours may always begin with thee, and through thee be ended. Through Christ our Lord. {℟.} Amen.
T: {℣.} Adjutórium nostrum in nómine Dómini. {℟.} Qui fecit cælum et terram.
 = {℣.} Our help is in the name of the Lord. {℟.} Who made heaven and earth.
R: Non dicatur *Fidélium ánimæ.*
 = *Fidélium ánimæ* is not said.
H: AD TERTIAM
 = TERCE
R: Dicto *Pater noster,* postquam versis vultibus ad Altare Fratres se signaverint, inchoëtur:
 = When the *Pater noster* has been said, and the brethren, turned towards the altar, have signed themselves, there is begun:
T: {Aña} Christus factus est. {Ps. 118, iii}
 = {Ant.} Christ became obedient… {Ps. 118, iii}
F: Psalmus 118, iii Legem pone | Psalm 118, iii
G: op-ten-h-ps-118-33-48 | Tonus VIII G
F: Textus et versio | Text and translation
T: Legem pone mihi, Dómine, viam iustificatiónum tuárum: * et exquíram eam semper.
 = Set before me for a law the way of thy justifications, O Lord: * and I will always seek after it.
T: Da mihi intelléctum, et scrutábor legem tuam: * et custódiam illam in toto corde meo.
 = Give me understanding, and I will search thy law; * and I will keep it with my whole heart.
T: Deduc me in sémitam mandatórum tuórum: * quia ipsam vólui.
 = Lead me into the path of thy commandments; * for this same I have desired.
T: Inclína cor meum in testimónia tua: * et non in avarítiam.
 = Incline my heart into thy testimonies * and not to covetousness.
T: Avérte óculos meos ne vídeant vanitátem: * in via tua vivífica me.
 = Turn away my eyes that they may not behold vanity: * quicken me in thy way.
T: Státue servo tuo elóquium tuum, * in timóre tuo.
 = Establish thy word to thy servant, * in thy fear.
T: Ámputa oppróbrium meum quod suspicátus sum: * quia iudícia tua iucúnda.
 = Turn away my reproach, which I have apprehended: * for thy judgments are delightful.
T: Ecce, concupívi mandáta tua: * in æquitáte tua vivífica me.
 = Behold I have longed after thy precepts: * quicken me in thy justice.
T: Et véniat super me misericórdia tua, Dómine: * salutáre tuum secúndum elóquium tuum.
 = Let thy mercy also come upon me, O Lord: * thy salvation according to thy word.
T: Et respondébo exprobrántibus mihi verbum: * quia sperávi in sermónibus tuis.
 = So shall I answer them that reproach me in any thing; * that I have trusted in thy words.
T: Et ne áuferas de ore meo verbum veritátis usquequáque: * quia in iudíciis tuis supersperávi.
 = And take not thou the word of truth utterly out of my mouth: * for in thy words, I have hoped exceedingly.
T: Et custódiam legem tuam semper: * in sǽculum et in sǽculum sǽculi.
 = So shall I always keep thy law, * for ever and ever.
T: Et ambulábam in latitúdine: * quia mandáta tua exquisívi.
 = And I walked at large: * because I have sought after thy commandments.
T: Et loquébar in testimóniis tuis in conspéctu regum: * et non confundébar.
 = And I spoke of thy testimonies before kings: * and I was not ashamed.
T: Et meditábar in mandátis tuis, * quæ diléxi.
 = I meditated also on thy commandments, * which I loved.
T: Et levávi manus meas ad mandáta tua, quæ diléxi: * et exercébar in iustificatiónibus tuis.
 = And I lifted up my hands to thy commandments, which I loved: * and I was exercised in thy justifications.
F/
F/
F: Psalmus 118, iv Memor esto | Psalm 118, iv
G: op-ten-h-ps-118-49-64 | Tonus VIII G
F: Textus et versio | Text and translation
T: Memor esto verbi tui servo tuo, * in quo mihi spem dedísti.
 = Be thou mindful of thy word to thy servant, * in which thou hast given me hope.
T: Hæc me consoláta est in humilitáte mea: * quia elóquium tuum vivificávit me.
 = This hath comforted me in my humiliation: * because thy word hath enlivened me.
T: Supérbi iníque agébant usquequáque: * a lege autem tua non declinávi.
 = The proud did iniquitously altogether: * but I declined not from thy law.
T: Memor fui iudiciórum tuórum a sǽculo, Dómine: * et consolátus sum.
 = I remembered, O Lord, thy judgments of old: * and I was comforted.
T: Deféctio ténuit me, * pro peccatóribus derelinquéntibus legem tuam.
 = A fainting hath taken hold of me, * because of the wicked that forsake thy law.
T: Cantábiles mihi erant iustificatiónes tuæ, * in loco peregrinatiónis meæ.
 = Thy justifications were the subject of my song, * in the place of my pilgrimage.
T: Memor fui nocte nóminis tui, Dómine: * et custodívi legem tuam.
 = In the night I have remembered thy name, O Lord: * and have kept thy law.
T: Hæc facta est mihi: * quia iustificatiónes tuas exquisívi.
 = This happened to me: * because I sought after thy justifications.
T: Pórtio mea, Dómine, * dixi custodíre legem tuam.
 = O Lord, my portion, * I have said, I would keep thy law.
T: Deprecátus sum fáciem tuam in toto corde meo: * miserére mei secúndum elóquium tuum.
 = I entreated thy face with all my heart: * have mercy on me according to thy word.
T: Cogitávi vias meas: * et convérti pedes meos in testimónia tua.
 = I have thought on my ways: * and turned my feet unto thy testimonies.
T: Parátus sum, et non sum turbátus: * ut custódiam mandáta tua.
 = I am ready, and am not troubled: * that I may keep thy commandments.
T: Funes peccatórum circumpléxi sunt me: * et legem tuam non sum oblítus.
 = The cords of the wicked have encompassed me: * but I have not forgotten thy law.
T: Média nocte surgébam ad confiténdum tibi, * super iudícia iustificatiónis tuæ.
 = I rose at midnight to give praise to thee; * for the judgments of thy justification.
T: Párticeps ego sum ómnium timéntium te: * et custodiéntium mandáta tua.
 = I am a partaker with all them that fear thee, * and that keep thy commandments.
T: Misericórdia tua, Dómine, plena est terra: * iustificatiónes tuas doce me.
 = The earth, O Lord, is full of thy mercy: * teach me thy justifications.
F/
F/
F: Psalmus 118, v Bonitátem fecísti | Psalm 118, v
G: op-ten-h-ps-118-65-80 | Tonus VIII G
F: Textus et versio | Text and translation
T: Bonitátem fecísti cum servo tuo, Dómine, * secúndum verbum tuum.
 = Thou hast done well with thy servant, O Lord, * according to thy word.
T: Bonitátem, et disciplínam, et sciéntiam doce me: * quia mandátis tuis crédidi.
 = Teach me goodness and discipline and knowledge; * for I have believed thy commandments.
T: Priúsquam humiliárer ego delíqui: * proptérea elóquium tuum custodívi.
 = Before I was humbled I offended; * therefore have I kept thy word.
T: Bonus es tu: * et in bonitáte tua doce me iustificatiónes tuas.
 = Thou art good; * and in thy goodness teach me thy justifications.
T: Multiplicáta est super me iníquitas superbórum: * ego autem in toto corde meo scrutábor mandáta tua.
 = The iniquity of the proud hath been multiplied over me: * but I will seek thy commandments with my whole heart.
T: Coagulátum est sicut lac cor eórum: * ego vero legem tuam meditátus sum.
 = Their heart is curdled like milk: * but I have meditated on thy law.
T: Bonum mihi quia humiliásti me: * ut discam iustificatiónes tuas.
 = It is good for me that thou hast humbled me, * that I may learn thy justifications.
T: Bonum mihi lex oris tui, * super míllia auri et argénti.
 = The law of thy mouth is good to me, * above thousands of gold and silver.
T: Manus tuæ fecérunt me, et plasmavérunt me: * da mihi intelléctum, et discam mandáta tua.
 = Thy hands have made me and formed me: * give me understanding, and I will learn thy commandments.
T: Qui timent te vidébunt me, et lætabúntur: * quia in verba tua supersperávi.
 = They that fear thee shall see me, and shall be glad: * because I have greatly hoped in thy words.
T: Cognóvi, Dómine, quia ǽquitas iudícia tua: * et in veritáte tua humiliásti me.
 = I know, O Lord, that thy judgments are equity: * and in thy truth thou hast humbled me.
T: Fiat misericórdia tua ut consolétur me, * secúndum elóquium tuum servo tuo.
 = O! let thy mercy be for my comfort, * according to thy word unto thy servant.
T: Véniant mihi miseratiónes tuæ, et vivam: * quia lex tua meditátio mea est.
 = Let thy tender mercies come unto me, and I shall live: * for thy law is my meditation.
T: Confundántur supérbi, quia iniúste iniquitátem fecérunt in me: * ego autem exercébor in mandátis tuis.
 = Let the proud be ashamed, because they have done unjustly towards me: * but I will be employed in thy commandments.
T: Convertántur mihi timéntes te: * et qui novérunt testimónia tua.
 = Let them that fear thee turn to me: * and they that know thy testimonies.
T: Fiat cor meum immaculátum in iustificatiónibus tuis, * ut non confúndar.
 = Let my heart be undefiled in thy justifications, * that I may not be confounded.
F/
F/
T: {Aña} Christus factus est pro nobis obédiens usque ad mortem, mortem autem crucis.
 = {Ant.} Christ became obedient for us unto death, even the death of the Cross.
G: op-ten-h-c01 | Antiphona · VIII
L: Oratio
 = Prayer
T: ^Réspice, quǽsumus, Dómine, super hanc famíliam tuam, pro qua Dóminus noster Jesus Christus non dubitávit mánibus tradi nocéntium, et crucis subíre torméntum.
 = Look down, we beseech thee, O Lord, upon this thy family, for which our Lord Jesus Christ did not hesitate to be delivered into the hands of the wicked, and to undergo the torment of the Cross.
H: AD SEXTAM
 = SEXT
R: Dicto *Pater noster,* postquam versis vultibus ad Altare Fratres se signaverint, inchoëtur:
 = When the *Pater noster* has been said, and the brethren, turned towards the altar, have signed themselves, there is begun:
T: {Aña} Christus factus est. {Ps. 118, vi}
 = {Ant.} Christ became obedient… {Ps. 118, vi}
F: Psalmus 118, vi Defécit | Psalm 118, vi
G: op-ten-h-ps-118-81-96 | Tonus VIII G
F: Textus et versio | Text and translation
T: Defécit in salutáre tuum ánima mea: * et in verbum tuum supersperávi.
 = My soul hath fainted after thy salvation: * and in thy word I have very much hoped.
T: Defecérunt óculi mei in elóquium tuum, * dicéntes: Quando consoláberis me?
 = My eyes have failed for thy word, * saying: When wilt thou comfort me?
T: Quia factus sum sicut uter in pruína: * iustificatiónes tuas non sum oblítus.
 = For I am become like a bottle in the frost: * I have not forgotten thy justifications.
T: Quot sunt dies servi tui? * quando fácies de persequéntibus me iudícium?
 = How many are the days of thy servant: * when wilt thou execute judgment on them that persecute me?
T: Narravérunt mihi iníqui fabulatiónes: * sed non ut lex tua.
 = The wicked have told me fables: * but not as thy law.
T: Ómnia mandáta tua véritas: * iníque persecúti sunt me, ádiuva me.
 = All thy statutes are truth: * they have persecuted me unjustly, do thou help me.
T: Paulo minus consummavérunt me in terra: * ego autem non derelíqui mandáta tua.
 = They had almost made an end of me upon earth: * but I have not forsaken thy commandments.
T: Secúndum misericórdiam tuam vivífica me: * et custódiam testimónia oris tui.
 = Quicken thou me according to thy mercy: * and I shall keep the testimonies of thy mouth.
T: In ætérnum, Dómine, * verbum tuum pérmanet in cælo.
 = For ever, O Lord, * thy word standeth firm in heaven.
T: In generatiónem et generatiónem véritas tua: * fundásti terram, et pérmanet.
 = Thy truth unto all generations: * thou hast founded the earth, and it continueth.
T: Ordinatióne tua persevérat dies: * quóniam ómnia sérviunt tibi.
 = By thy ordinance the day goeth on: * for all things serve thee.
T: Nisi quod lex tua meditátio mea est: * tunc forte periíssem in humilitáte mea.
 = Unless thy law had been my meditation, * I had then perhaps perished in my abjection.
T: In ætérnum non oblivíscar iustificatiónes tuas: * quia in ipsis vivificásti me.
 = Thy justifications I will never forget: * for by them thou hast given me life.
T: Tuus sum ego, salvum me fac: * quóniam iustificatiónes tuas exquisívi.
 = I am thine, save thou me: * for I have sought thy justifications.
T: Me exspectavérunt peccatóres ut pérderent me: * testimónia tua intelléxi.
 = The wicked have waited for me to destroy me: * but I have understood thy testimonies.
T: Omnis consummatiónis vidi finem: * latum mandátum tuum nimis.
 = I have seen an end of all perfection: * thy commandment is exceeding broad.
F/
F/
F: Psalmus 118, vii Quómodo diléxi | Psalm 118, vii
G: op-ten-h-ps-118-97-112 | Tonus VIII G
F: Textus et versio | Text and translation
T: Quómodo diléxi legem tuam, Dómine? * tota die meditátio mea est.
 = How have I loved thy law, O Lord! * it is my meditation all the day.
T: Super inimícos meos prudéntem me fecísti mandáto tuo: * quia in ætérnum mihi est.
 = Through thy commandment, thou hast made me wiser than my enemies: * for it is ever with me.
T: Super omnes docéntes me intelléxi: * quia testimónia tua meditátio mea est.
 = I have understood more than all my teachers: * because thy testimonies are my meditation.
T: Super senes intelléxi: * quia mandáta tua quæsívi.
 = I have had understanding above ancients: * because I have sought thy commandments.
T: Ab omni via mala prohíbui pedes meos: * ut custódiam verba tua.
 = I have restrained my feet from every evil way: * that I may keep thy words.
T: A iudíciis tuis non declinávi: * quia tu legem posuísti mihi.
 = I have not declined from thy judgments, * because thou hast set me a law.
T: Quam dúlcia fáucibus meis elóquia tua, * super mel ori meo!
 = How sweet are thy words to my palate! * more than honey to my mouth.
T: A mandátis tuis intelléxi: * proptérea odívi omnem viam iniquitátis.
 = By thy commandments I have had understanding: * therefore have I hated every way of iniquity.
T: Lucérna pédibus meis verbum tuum, * et lumen sémitis meis.
 = Thy word is a lamp to my feet, * and a light to my paths.
T: Iurávi, et státui * custodíre iudícia iustítiæ tuæ.
 = I have sworn and am determined * to keep the judgments of thy justice.
T: Humiliátus sum usquequáque, Dómine: * vivífica me secúndum verbum tuum.
 = I have been humbled, O Lord, exceedingly: * quicken thou me according to thy word.
T: Voluntária oris mei beneplácita fac, Dómine: * et iudícia tua doce me.
 = The free offerings of my mouth make acceptable, O Lord: * and teach me thy judgments.
T: Ánima mea in mánibus meis semper: * et legem tuam non sum oblítus.
 = My soul is continually in my hands: * and I have not forgotten thy law.
T: Posuérunt peccatóres láqueum mihi: * et de mandátis tuis non errávi.
 = Sinners have laid a snare for me: * but I have not erred from thy precepts.
T: Hereditáte acquisívi testimónia tua in ætérnum: * quia exsultátio cordis mei sunt.
 = I have purchased thy testimonies for an inheritance for ever: * because they are the joy of my heart.
T: Inclinávi cor meum ad faciéndas iustificatiónes tuas in ætérnum, * propter retributiónem.
 = I have inclined my heart to do thy justifications for ever, * for the reward.
F/
F/
F: Psalmus 118, viii Iníquos ódio hábui | Psalm 118, viii
G: op-ten-h-ps-118-113-128 | Tonus VIII G
F: Textus et versio | Text and translation
T: Iníquos ódio hábui: * et legem tuam diléxi.
 = I have hated the unjust: * and I have loved thy law.
T: Adiútor et suscéptor meus es tu: * et in verbum tuum supersperávi.
 = Thou art my helper and my protector: * and in thy word I have greatly hoped.
T: Declináte a me, malígni: * et scrutábor mandáta Dei mei.
 = Depart from me, ye malignant: * and I will search the commandments of my God.
T: Súscipe me secúndum elóquium tuum, et vivam: * et non confúndas me ab exspectatióne mea.
 = Uphold me according to thy word, and I shall live: * and let me not be confounded in my expectation.
T: Ádiuva me, et salvus ero: * et meditábor in iustificatiónibus tuis semper.
 = Help me, and I shall be saved: * and I will meditate always on thy justifications.
T: Sprevísti omnes discedéntes a iudíciis tuis: * quia iniústa cogitátio eórum.
 = Thou hast despised all them that fall off from thy judgments; * for their thought is unjust.
T: Prævaricántes reputávi omnes peccatóres terræ: * ídeo diléxi testimónia tua.
 = I have accounted all the sinners of the earth prevaricators: * therefore have I loved thy testimonies.
T: Confíge timóre tuo carnes meas: * a iudíciis enim tuis tímui.
 = Pierce thou my flesh with thy fear: * for I am afraid of thy judgments.
T: Feci iudícium et iustítiam: * non tradas me calumniántibus me.
 = I have done judgment and justice: * give me not up to them that slander me.
T: Súscipe servum tuum in bonum: * non calumniéntur me supérbi.
 = Uphold thy servant unto good: * let not the proud calumniate me.
T: Óculi mei defecérunt in salutáre tuum: * et in elóquium iustítiæ tuæ.
 = My eyes have fainted after thy salvation: * and for the word of thy justice.
T: Fac cum servo tuo secúndum misericórdiam tuam: * et iustificatiónes tuas doce me.
 = Deal with thy servant according to thy mercy: * and teach me thy justifications.
T: Servus tuus sum ego: * da mihi intelléctum, ut sciam testimónia tua.
 = I am thy servant: * give me understanding that I may know thy testimonies.
T: Tempus faciéndi, Dómine: * dissipavérunt legem tuam.
 = It is time, O Lord, to do: * they have dissipated thy law.
T: Ídeo diléxi mandáta tua, * super aurum et topázion.
 = Therefore have I loved thy commandments * above gold and the topaz.
T: Proptérea ad ómnia mandáta tua dirigébar: * omnem viam iníquam ódio hábui.
 = Therefore was I directed to all thy commandments: * I have hated all wicked ways.
F/
F/
T: {Aña} Christus factus est pro nobis obédiens usque ad mortem, mortem autem crucis.
 = {Ant.} Christ became obedient for us unto death, even the death of the Cross.
G: op-ten-h-c01 | Antiphona · VIII
L: Oratio
 = Prayer
T: ^Réspice, quǽsumus, Dómine, super hanc famíliam tuam, pro qua Dóminus noster Jesus Christus non dubitávit mánibus tradi nocéntium, et crucis subíre torméntum.
 = Look down, we beseech thee, O Lord, upon this thy family, for which our Lord Jesus Christ did not hesitate to be delivered into the hands of the wicked, and to undergo the torment of the Cross.
H: AD NONAM
 = NONE
R: Dicto *Pater noster,* postquam versis vultibus ad Altare Fratres se signaverint, inchoëtur:
 = When the *Pater noster* has been said, and the brethren, turned towards the altar, have signed themselves, there is begun:
T: {Aña} Christus factus est. {Ps. 118, ix}
 = {Ant.} Christ became obedient… {Ps. 118, ix}
F: Psalmus 118, ix Mirabília | Psalm 118, ix
G: op-ten-h-ps-118-129-144 | Tonus VIII G
F: Textus et versio | Text and translation
T: Mirabília testimónia tua: * ídeo scrutáta est ea ánima mea.
 = Thy testimonies are wonderful: * therefore my soul hath sought them.
T: Declarátio sermónum tuórum illúminat: * et intelléctum dat párvulis.
 = The declaration of thy words giveth light: * and giveth understanding to little ones.
T: Os meum apérui, et attráxi spíritum: * quia mandáta tua desiderábam.
 = I opened my mouth, and panted: * because I longed for thy commandments.
T: Áspice in me, et miserére mei, * secúndum iudícium diligéntium nomen tuum.
 = Look thou upon me, and have mercy on me, * according to the judgment of them that love thy name.
T: Gressus meos dírige secúndum elóquium tuum: * et non dominétur mei omnis iniustítia.
 = Direct my steps according to thy word: * and let no iniquity have dominion over me.
T: Rédime me a calúmniis hóminum: * ut custódiam mandáta tua.
 = Redeem me from the calumnies of men: * that I may keep thy commandments.
T: Fáciem tuam illúmina super servum tuum: * et doce me iustificatiónes tuas.
 = Make thy face to shine upon thy servant: * and teach me thy justifications.
T: Éxitus aquárum deduxérunt óculi mei: * quia non custodiérunt legem tuam.
 = My eyes have sent forth springs of water: * because they have not kept thy law.
T: Iustus es, Dómine: * et rectum iudícium tuum.
 = Thou art just, O Lord: * and thy judgment is right.
T: Mandásti iustítiam testimónia tua: * et veritátem tuam nimis.
 = Thou hast commanded justice thy testimonies: * and thy truth exceedingly.
T: Tabéscere me fecit zelus meus: * quia oblíti sunt verba tua inimíci mei.
 = My zeal hath made me pine away: * because my enemies forgot thy words.
T: Ignítum elóquium tuum veheménter: * et servus tuus diléxit illud.
 = Thy word is exceedingly refined: * and thy servant hath loved it.
T: Adolescéntulus sum ego et contémptus: * iustificatiónes tuas non sum oblítus.
 = I am very young and despised; but * I forget not thy justifications.
T: Iustítia tua, iustítia in ætérnum: * et lex tua véritas.
 = Thy justice is justice for ever: * and thy law is the truth.
T: Tribulátio, et angústia invenérunt me: * mandáta tua meditátio mea est.
 = Trouble and anguish have found me: * thy commandments are my meditation.
T: Ǽquitas testimónia tua in ætérnum: * intelléctum da mihi, et vivam.
 = Thy testimonies are justice for ever: * give me understanding, and I shall live.
F/
F/
F: Psalmus 118, x Clamávi | Psalm 118, x
G: op-ten-h-ps-118-145-160 | Tonus VIII G
F: Textus et versio | Text and translation
T: Clamávi in toto corde meo, exáudi me, Dómine: * iustificatiónes tuas requíram.
 = I cried with my whole heart, hear me, O Lord: * I will seek thy justifications.
T: Clamávi ad te, salvum me fac: * ut custódiam mandáta tua.
 = I cried unto thee, save me: * that I may keep thy commandments.
T: Prævéni in maturitáte, et clamávi: * quia in verba tua supersperávi.
 = I prevented the dawning of the day, and cried: * because in thy words I very much hoped.
T: Prævenérunt óculi mei ad te dilúculo: * ut meditárer elóquia tua.
 = My eyes to thee have prevented the morning: * that I might meditate on thy words.
T: Vocem meam audi secúndum misericórdiam tuam, Dómine: * et secúndum iudícium tuum vivífica me.
 = Hear thou my voice, O Lord, according to thy mercy: * and quicken me according to thy judgment.
T: Appropinquavérunt persequéntes me iniquitáti: * a lege autem tua longe facti sunt.
 = They that persecute me have drawn nigh to iniquity; * but they are gone far off from thy law.
T: Prope es tu, Dómine: * et omnes viæ tuæ véritas.
 = Thou art near, O Lord: * and all thy ways are truth.
T: Inítio cognóvi de testimóniis tuis: * quia in ætérnum fundásti ea.
 = I have known from the beginning concerning thy testimonies: * that thou hast founded them for ever.
T: Vide humilitátem meam, et éripe me: * quia legem tuam non sum oblítus.
 = See my humiliation and deliver me: * for I have not forgotten thy law.
T: Iúdica iudícium meum, et rédime me: * propter elóquium tuum vivífica me.
 = Judge my judgment and redeem me: * quicken thou me for thy word’s sake.
T: Longe a peccatóribus salus: * quia iustificatiónes tuas non exquisiérunt.
 = Salvation is far from sinners; * because they have not sought thy justifications.
T: Misericórdiæ tuæ multæ, Dómine: * secúndum iudícium tuum vivífica me.
 = Many, O Lord, are thy mercies: * quicken me according to thy judgment.
T: Multi qui persequúntur me, et tríbulant me: * a testimóniis tuis non declinávi.
 = Many are they that persecute me, and afflict me; * but I have not declined from thy testimonies.
T: Vidi prævaricántes, et tabescébam: * quia elóquia tua non custodiérunt.
 = I beheld the transgressors, and I pined away; * because they kept not thy word.
T: Vide quóniam mandáta tua diléxi, Dómine: * in misericórdia tua vivífica me.
 = Behold I have loved thy commandments, O Lord; * quicken me thou in thy mercy.
T: Princípium verbórum tuórum, véritas: * in ætérnum ómnia iudícia iustítiæ tuæ.
 = The beginning of thy words is truth: * all the judgments of thy justice are for ever.
F/
F/
F: Psalmus 118, xi Príncipes | Psalm 118, xi
G: op-ten-h-ps-118-161-176 | Tonus VIII G
F: Textus et versio | Text and translation
T: Príncipes persecúti sunt me gratis: * et a verbis tuis formidávit cor meum.
 = Princes have persecuted me without cause: * and my heart hath been in awe of thy words.
T: Lætábor ego super elóquia tua: * sicut qui invénit spólia multa.
 = I will rejoice at thy words, * as one that hath found great spoil.
T: Iniquitátem ódio hábui, et abominátus sum: * legem autem tuam diléxi.
 = I have hated and abhorred iniquity; * but I have loved thy law.
T: Sépties in die laudem dixi tibi, * super iudícia iustítiæ tuæ.
 = Seven times a day I have given praise to thee, * for the judgments of thy justice.
T: Pax multa diligéntibus legem tuam: * et non est illis scándalum.
 = Much peace have they that love thy law, * and to them there is no stumbling-block.
T: Exspectábam salutáre tuum, Dómine: * et mandáta tua diléxi.
 = I looked for thy salvation, O Lord: * and I loved thy commandments.
T: Custodívit ánima mea testimónia tua: * et diléxit ea veheménter.
 = My soul hath kept thy testimonies: * and hath loved them exceedingly.
T: Servávi mandáta tua, et testimónia tua: * quia omnes viæ meæ in conspéctu tuo.
 = I have kept thy commandments and thy testimonies: * because all my ways are in thy sight.
T: Appropínquet deprecátio mea in conspéctu tuo, Dómine: * iuxta elóquium tuum da mihi intelléctum.
 = Let my supplication, O Lord, come near in thy sight: * give me understanding according to thy word.
T: Intret postulátio mea in conspéctu tuo: * secúndum elóquium tuum éripe me.
 = Let my request come in before thee; * deliver thou me according to thy word.
T: Eructábunt lábia mea hymnum, * cum docúeris me iustificatiónes tuas.
 = My lips shall utter a hymn, * when thou shalt teach me thy justifications.
T: Pronuntiábit lingua mea elóquium tuum: * quia ómnia mandáta tua ǽquitas.
 = My tongue shall pronounce thy word: * because all thy commandments are justice.
T: Fiat manus tua ut salvet me: * quóniam mandáta tua elégi.
 = Let thy hand be with me to save me; * for I have chosen thy precepts.
T: Concupívi salutáre tuum, Dómine: * et lex tua meditátio mea est.
 = I have longed for thy salvation, O Lord; * and thy law is my meditation.
T: Vivet ánima mea, et laudábit te: * et iudícia tua adiuvábunt me.
 = My soul shall live and shall praise thee: * and thy judgments shall help me.
T: Errávi, sicut ovis, quæ périit: * quære servum tuum, quia mandáta tua non sum oblítus.
 = I have gone astray like a sheep that is lost: * seek thy servant, because I have not forgotten thy commandments.
F/
F/
T: {Aña} Christus factus est pro nobis obédiens usque ad mortem, mortem autem crucis.
 = {Ant.} Christ became obedient for us unto death, even the death of the Cross.
G: op-ten-h-c01 | Antiphona · VIII
L: Oratio
 = Prayer
T: ^Réspice, quǽsumus, Dómine, super hanc famíliam tuam, pro qua Dóminus noster Jesus Christus non dubitávit mánibus tradi nocéntium, et crucis subíre torméntum.
 = Look down, we beseech thee, O Lord, upon this thy family, for which our Lord Jesus Christ did not hesitate to be delivered into the hands of the wicked, and to undergo the torment of the Cross.
R: Fratres privatas Missas non celebrent, sed omnes in Missa conventuali communicent. Sacrista vero ante Nonam omnia altaria detegat, præter majus, (quod dum Vesperæ cantantur detegatur, una sola mappa super ipsum usque post finem Missæ relicta, qua finita removeatur) et usque ad horam Tertiam Sabbati sequentis detecta remaneant, præter majus, quod in crastino hora Officii cooperiatur.
 = The brethren do not celebrate private Masses, but all receive Communion at the conventual Mass. Before None the Sacristan strips all the altars except the high altar (which is stripped while Vespers are sung, a single cloth being left on it until after the end of the Mass, and removed when the Mass is over); and they remain bare until the hour of Terce on the following Saturday, except the high altar, which is covered on the morrow at the time of the Office.
